# The record checks: what the repository must be true of ITSELF.
#
# Port of archive/bootstrap-rs/heroes-cli/tests/milestones.rs, five checks that read no
# compiler output at all — they read the tree. Panel 085 named
# `milestones.rs ×5` among the tests with no successor whatever, and this
# is the module that answers it.
#
# The rule they all serve is CLAUDE.md §14: milestone identifiers are
# NAMES, not numbers, and an identifier never reaches a diagnostic. What
# the rename bought is an invariant, and without these checks it is a
# one-time cleanup that rots on the next commit.

use report
use shell
use strings

# Dated records: a panel, a journal, a measurement, a defect report, a
# story beat, a golden fixture, the design log. They KEEP the identifiers
# they were written with, and appending to one uses that file's vocabulary
# (§14) — so they are outside the walk rather than exceptions inside it.
#
# The rest are not records but noise: `vendor/` holds third-party byte
# tables whose BPE bigrams contain `M6`-shaped pairs, and
# `editors/vscode/icons/` holds SVG path data where `M14` is a moveto
# command.
#
# **`target`, `build`, `.git` and `.claude/worktrees` were on this list until
# 2026-08-19 and are not any more**, which is not an oversight: every one of them
# is gitignored, and the walk asks GIT which files are this project's rather than
# asking the filesystem what is there (`shell.project_files`). A list of
# exclusions is a premise about which junk exists — it expired the day a parallel
# session wrote a gitignored TLS key into `site/.cache/` and both record walks
# read `M3KN` out of its base64. What remains here is what git cannot know: files
# it tracks that are RECORDS.
# The one heading this project's sittings answer under. It is a CONSTANT because
# the check and the message that explains the check must not be able to disagree
# about it — the shape CLAUDE.md §11 calls a premise that expires in silence.
constant VERDICT_HEADING: str
    "\n## Author's verdict"

# WHERE THE SITTINGS LIVE, in one place, for VERDICT_HEADING's reason: the check,
# the arithmetic that strips this prefix and the message that explains the check
# must not be able to disagree about it. The literal stood in three functions and
# in a `.len()` call on a fourth line, which is four places one move could have
# left disagreeing — and `verdicts` had no floor to notice (see its own comment).
constant SITTINGS: str
    "docs/panel/"

# **`docs/work/DONE.md` joined this list on 2026-09-06**, and it was missing for
# the ten days it has existed. CLAUDE.md §3 calls it *"the record — append-only,
# never rewritten (§14), and read by grep rather than by eye"*, which is the
# definition of every other entry here; it was simply never added when the lists
# were re-cut and `docs/debrief/` went away on 2026-08-26. Measured the day it was found: the
# citation check, widened to the whole tree, wanted **34** repairs inside that
# file, every one of them a path a dated entry had cited correctly on the day it
# was written. A record does not follow a tree that moves; that is the whole
# reason §14 forbids rewriting one, and an instrument that asks it to is asking
# for the contract to be broken.
constant RECORD: [str]
    [
        "docs/panel"
        "docs/journal"
        "docs/measurements"
        "docs/book"
        "tests/golden"
        "tests/emission"
        "DESIGN-LOG.md"
        "docs/log"
        "docs/work/DONE.md"
        "docs/done"
        "docs/contract"
        "vendor"
        "editors/vscode/icons"
        "archive"
    ]

# **`site/log.html` was on that list and is not any more** (deleted 2026-08-18), and it is
# rather than repointed. The entry stopped matching anything the day the site's
# pages moved under `site/public/`, and it matched nothing again when they moved
# to `site/src/html/`: measured 2026-08-28, `site/log.html` selects **0** of the
# repository's tracked paths. It was also never needed. The claim, falsifiable by
# one command: **the build log names no numbered milestone in either edition** —
# measured the same day, 0 occurrences in `site/src/html/log.html` and 0 in
# `site/src/html/it/log.html`, because every entry there is written `M-<name>`.
#
# Repointing it would have restored a line that has never fired, over exactly the
# surface §14 cares most about: the site is user-visible output, and that is where
# an identifier must never appear. A dead exemption is not free — it reads as a
# statement that the log is allowed to breach the rule, and the next person to add
# an entry would believe it.
#
# **`docs/reasoning` left the list on 2026-09-04 for the opposite reason: the tree
# it selected was deleted the same commit**, by author instruction retiring the
# reasoning notes into `docs/work/DONE.md`. The seven notes were records and the
# entry was live — it is removed because there is nothing to exempt, not because
# it was wrong, and `records/exemptions` below is what obliged the two halves to
# move together. The text is at `git show 365fe929:docs/reasoning/`.

# Files git tracks that NOBODY WRITES. Not records, which are prose somebody
# meant; artifacts, whose bytes are chosen by a tool.
#
# `site/package-lock.json` is here because base64 and this check disagree about
# what a word boundary is. `opens_at` excludes a letter, a digit, `_` and `-`
# before the `M`, which is what makes `sha512-M3...` safe. It does not exclude
# `+` or `/`, and those two are IN THE BASE64 ALPHABET, so an integrity hash
# containing `+M3` or `/M9` reads as a milestone identifier. Measured 2026-08-28
# on the 232,880-byte lockfile this repository now carries: **4 occurrences**,
# `/M9`, `/M7`, `+M3` and `/M6`, every one of them inside an `integrity` field,
# and `M93B` is the one the check reported. `named` finds 0, so only this half of
# the rule is affected.
#
# The falsifiable claim: **no file in this list is written by a person, so no
# identifier in one is a claim about this project.** The day that stops being
# true of an entry, it belongs in RECORD or nowhere.
#
# Exact match, never a prefix. RECORD names trees because a record directory is
# a record all the way down; a generated file is one file, and a prefix here
# would quietly exempt a directory somebody adds later.
constant GENERATED: [str]
    [
        "site/package-lock.json"
    ]

# The blessed emissions: 5.4 MB of generated C, derived from the
# programs under `tests/golden/` and `examples/`. Reading it here
# would scan a megabyte-scale COPY of text this walk already reads
# at its source, and a milestone name in a program would be
# reported twice — once truly, once about a file nobody writes. The
# guard the walk would have given (`c_files` below) is stronger in
# `suite_emission.hero`: a `.c` in there that no program claims is
# a failure by name, which is more than "a C file lives outside the
# directories that own C".

# **The archived bootstrap is a frozen tree, not a living file.** `archive/bootstrap-rs/`
# became `archive/bootstrap-rs/` at M-bootstrap-archive; nothing builds it,
# nothing runs its tests, and its 25,482 lines of Rust name every milestone
# this project ever had under the spelling it had then. Walking it would
# report five years of history as today's claims — the same argument that
# kept `.claude/worktrees` out when it was a list of exclusions: a second
# tree's names are not this tree's.

# Files whose milestone identifiers are QUOTATIONS of the retired
# spelling, kept on purpose so a test can prove the legacy form is still
# recognised. `tests/harness/` is here for the same reason the Rust file
# is: this module quotes `M0` and `M5b` in its own assertions.
constant QUOTES_THE_OLD_SPELLING: [str]
    [
        "docs/work/DONE.md"
        "docs/ROADMAP.md"
        "CLAUDE.md"
        "tests/harness/suite_records.hero"
    ]

# Three entries left this list at M-bootstrap-archive — the bootstrap's
# `emit/tests/gate.rs`, `tests/milestones.rs` and `cli/help.rs`. They are
# inside `archive/` now, which RECORD skips whole, so an exemption for them
# would be a line that cannot fire.
#
# **The record was `docs/debrief/QUEUE.md` until 2026-08-26.** The entry is a
# SUFFIX match (`quoting` below), so the rename would have silently stopped
# matching and every `M0` and `M5b` in a 486 KB append-only record would have
# been reported as a §14 breach — the check firing loudly about the wrong
# thing, which is worse than not firing.

# The names deliberately written without being milestones: a runner-up
# § The names records as refused, so the reasoning survives the decision.
#
# **`M-foreign-ownership` joined it 2026-09-04**, and it joined by making this
# check fire. Panel 109's `owned` mark took the id `M-declared-freer`, and both
# the ROADMAP row and the `SCHEDULED.md` item say **why it is not the obvious
# name**: the `ptr owned` half is refused under a standing veto with measured
# return conditions, so an id that annexed the whole ownership area would make a
# claim a later milestone could falsify (CLAUDE.md §14). Writing that reasoning
# down is what this list is for — a refused name is written *once*, in the
# sentence that refuses it, and nowhere else.
#
# **`M-wasm-playground` and `M-browser-compiler` joined it 2026-09-06**, both
# runners-up to `M-online-compiler`, and they are the first pair refused for the
# same reason as each other: the milestone's opening question **is** which engine
# runs the visitor's program, so either id would assert the answer the sitting
# exists to give. `wasm` is also owed to the later half of Part 7 item 15
# (`design.md:2645`), which is the ordinary §14 rule — an area a later milestone
# needs stays free.
# **`M-adversarial-corpus` and `M-compiler-survival` joined it 2026-09-06**, the
# two runners-up to `M-generated-programs`, refused for the two halves of §14's
# naming rule. The first names an AREA three milestones already share —
# `M-program-corpus`, `M-corpus-coverage` and `M-corpus-depth` — and the area must
# stay free for whatever touches it next. The second states a PROMISE, *the
# compiler survives*, which a later milestone can falsify and which an id may
# therefore never claim; it is `M-language`'s refusal again, one department over.
#
# **`M-debugger`, `M-stopped-values` and `M-frame-values` joined it 2026-09-06**,
# the three names not taken for `M-typed-inspection`, and they are refused on three
# different halves of §14. `M-debugger` is ONE WORD, so it appropriates a topic, and
# it would claim the line-level debugging a closed milestone already delivered —
# M-selfhost-port carried `-g` in as a defect on 2026-08-17 so that lldb could break
# on a `.hero` line at all — while leaving no name for the `heroes dap`
# `docs/ROADMAP.md` already reserves under CLAUDE.md §10's stopping rule.
# `M-stopped-values` ANNEXES ANOTHER SITTING: `print` and `assert` render values
# too, and a `to_str` derived over a record's fields is `M-reflection-verdict`'s own
# question. `M-frame-values` names the COMPILER's word for the place rather than the
# author's word for the thing, and what is delivered is the value seen, not the
# frame. The name taken is the one the record already used, twice, and both times as
# the non-goal it retires (`design.md:594`).
constant REFUSED: [str]
    [
        "M-language"
        "M-function-labels"
        "M-foreign-ownership"
        "M-wasm-playground"
        "M-browser-compiler"
        "M-adversarial-corpus"
        "M-compiler-survival"
        "M-debugger"
        "M-stopped-values"
        "M-frame-values"
        "M-cstr-escape"
        "M-use-after-free"
        "M-borrowed-pointer"
        "M-pinned-bytes"
        "M-fourth-platform"
        "M-arm-linux"
    ]

# Measured 2026-08-25: 40 rows in the ROADMAP's alias table (39 until
# M-package-layout was scheduled — author decision 2026-08-25, `/decide`).
# The count is asserted rather than derived on purpose — it is the tripwire
# that makes a SILENTLY DROPPED row fail, which a set comparison alone
# would not. It fired on the row that added it, which is the intended
# direction: a new milestone reaches this number before it reaches a tag.
#
# **41 → 43 on 2026-09-02**, and it fired again exactly as designed: two
# milestones were scheduled in `docs/work/SCHEDULED.md (retired 2026-09-12)` (`M-corpus-coverage`
# and `M-selfhost-nesting`, both by author instruction) and this check named
# them both as *a name with no row in the alias table* in the same run that
# filed them. A milestone reaches this number before it reaches a tag, and
# now before it reaches a step.
#
# **43 → 44 on 2026-09-02**, `M-interpolation-verdict`, scheduled by author
# instruction: a milestone whose deliverable is a RULING rather than a feature
# reaches this number the same way any other does. This one arrives before it
# has a step, a tag or a line of code, which is the direction the count was
# written for.
#
# **44 → 45 on 2026-09-03**, `M-robustness-guards`, opened by author instruction
# out of a `/decide` sitting. The sibling check `records/names` fired first, in
# the commit that scheduled six items under the name before the ROADMAP had a
# row for it — the order CLAUDE.md §14 asks for, read by an instrument.
#
# **45 → 46 on 2026-09-03**, `M-corpus-depth`, scheduled by author instruction
# from a plan measured and approved the same day, its step 0 landing with the
# row. The planning found the same milestone open in `SCHEDULED.md` and closed
# in `DESIGN-LOG.md` a day after its close — two lists disagreeing about one
# fact — which is the shape a count that must be moved by hand exists to catch.
#
# **46 → 48 on 2026-09-03**, `M-core-packages` and `M-web-framework`, scheduled
# by author instruction out of a reasoning session the same evening: two ids that
# reach this number before either has a step, a tag or a sitting — the sitting is
# at the first one's opening — as `M-interpolation-verdict` did. Two rows in one
# move because they are two deliverables (CLAUDE.md §14): the packages, and the
# framework that composes them.
#
# **48 → 55 on 2026-09-03**, later the same evening, out of a reasoning session
# that re-read the whole chain for what it lacked (`DESIGN-LOG.md:539`): four
# rulings on the language (`M-closures-verdict`, `M-reflection-verdict`,
# `M-deferral-ledger`, with `M-interpolation-verdict` moved up beside them), one
# runtime guard (`M-panic-location`), one instrument (`M-thesis-harness`), one
# tool (`M-doc-generator`) and the way in (`M-install-channels`). Seven ids that
# reach this number before any has a step, a tag or a sitting, by author
# instruction, each named for its deliverable (CLAUDE.md §14).
#
# **55 → 56 on 2026-09-03**, `M-discard-refusal`, out of the `/decide` sitting
# that ran beside M-corpus-depth step 4: `_ =` on a fallible value becomes a
# compile error (author answer `3a`). A new id rather than a step of the corpus
# milestone that found it — the instrument is `heroes mutate`'s, the deliverable
# is a language rule — and this count is what noticed the id was in
# `SCHEDULED.md` with no row here, which is this check's whole job. It went red
# twice in one minute, once for the missing row and once for this number, and
# both are the instrument working rather than failing.
#
# **56 → 59 on 2026-09-04**, by author instruction — *"fix SCHEDULED too, so that
# every item is attached to a roadmap step that is still to be done; if you cannot
# find one, create it"* — and the three ids are what the instruction cost:
# `M-declared-freer` (panel 109's `owned` mark, whose item was scheduled at
# `M-ffi-ladder`, closed 2026-08-12), `M-thread-stacks` (panel 107's per-thread
# guard and the Windows stack flag, whose two items named no milestone at all) and
# `M-check-completeness` (the two items parked on a `grep` since 2026-08-16).
# **The shape this count exists to catch is the one that produced all three**: an
# item may name a home that is closed, or no home at all, and read as scheduled
# either way — twelve of twenty-nine did. A fourth row entered the chain in the
# same commit and is NOT in this number, because `M-discard-refusal` already had
# its alias row and was missing only the chain row, which is the order §14 asks
# for and the direction this check reads.
#
# **59 -> 60 on 2026-09-05**, one id, `M-c-callbacks`, out of `docs/panel/111`.
# The sitting refused the proposal it was convened to adopt and split the
# callback permission out of M-isolated-threads, because three seats found it is
# bought entirely by §1.11 and CLAUDE.md §12's FFI-completeness instruction and
# needs no thread at all. **This check fired on it within a minute of the
# sitting closing**, before the name had reached the alias table, which is the
# order §14 asks for and the third time this count has caught its own case.
# The id has no chain row: placing one is the author's (`SCHEDULED.md`).
#
# **60 -> 61 on 2026-09-06**, `M-online-compiler`, scheduled by author
# instruction: the compiler reached without installing anything, for the visitor
# who will not run even the one clang line M-install-channels builds every
# channel from. **It half-reverses a recorded refusal, and the row says which
# half** — `DESIGN-LOG.md:539` refused *a web playground* on 2026-09-03 because
# wasm cannot call native C libraries, which is an argument about a Heroes
# program compiled to wasm and not about the compiler running in a browser. The
# id enters with its chain row in the same commit, so this number is moved by
# hand here rather than read off a red run, and `REFUSED` gains two names in the
# same move.
#
# **61 -> 62 on 2026-09-06**, `M-generated-programs`, scheduled by author
# instruction: a generator that writes valid Heroes and knows the answer before
# the compiler is asked. **It is the first id whose subject is this repository's
# own instruments pointing the other way** — `heroes mutate` takes programs that
# check clean and makes one plausible mistake per site, so everything here is
# aimed at wrong programs, and of the fourteen defect entries in
# `docs/work/DONE.md` every `found by` field names a person. The id enters with
# its chain row in the same commit, so this number is moved by hand, and
# `REFUSED` gains two names in the same move.
#
# **62 -> 63 on 2026-09-06**, `M-typed-inspection`, scheduled by author
# instruction: a stopped Heroes program shows Heroes values instead of the C
# names underneath them. **It is the first id whose subject is a promise this
# repository makes and does not test** — `docs/ROADMAP.md:500` says a golden runs
# lldb in batch mode, and that golden has been in
# `archive/bootstrap-rs/` since M-bootstrap-archive on 2026-08-19, where nothing
# builds it. The id enters with its chain row in the same commit, so this number
# is moved by hand, and `REFUSED` gains THREE names in the same move, which is one
# more than the two previous inserts: the author was offered three candidates and
# the two not taken are refused here beside the name that never reached them.
# **The sentence above said `design.md:616` and `docs/ROADMAP.md:452` until
# 2026-09-10**, and both were wrong: design.md never claimed the golden at all
# (`grep -n "batch mode" design.md` is empty; its lldb sentences are `:614`,
# `:635-636`, `:664` and `:2886`), and the ROADMAP's claim moved to `:500`. A
# line-number citation is exactly what `records/citations` cannot judge — it reads
# a PATH claim — so this pair sat here being blessed by the check beside it, which
# is the shape the exemptions rule exists to catch one level up.
#
# **63 -> 64 on 2026-09-08**, `M-open-repository`, scheduled by author
# instruction: the repository stops being private. **It is the first id inserted
# ahead of an OPEN milestone rather than appended after the last scheduled one**
# — it takes row 38 and `M-discard-refusal` goes back to `scheduled`, which is
# honest because not a line of that milestone had landed. The insert renumbers
# rows 38 to 59 into 39 to 60, so this file is not the only instrument the move
# touches: eleven citations of a chain row by number inside `docs/ROADMAP.md`
# were converted to the milestone's NAME instead of to a new number, on the
# 2026-09-03 precedent that a reorder moves a number and never a name — and two
# of those eleven were already pointing at the wrong row before this milestone
# existed. The id enters with its chain row in the same commit, so this number
# is moved by hand here rather than read off a red run.
#
# **64 -> 65 on 2026-09-09**, `M-cstr-lifetime`, placed FIRST and alone by author
# decision out of three the coordinator recommended: a `cstr` may not outlive the
# `str` it was lent from, which is defect 022, a use-after-free that builds with
# zero diagnostics and answers wrong at exit 0. **This number was read off a red
# run rather than moved by hand**, which is the direction the tripwire was written
# for and the first time it has been used that way: the row went in, `records/table`
# said *"the alias table has 65 rows, not 64"*, and the constant followed the
# instrument instead of the instrument following a guess. `REFUSED` gains two
# names in the same move, `M-cstr-escape` and `M-use-after-free` — the first names
# the symptom where §14 asks for the deliverable, and the second annexes a class
# the language can reach by other routes, which would leave the next one nameless.
#
# **65 -> 66 on 2026-09-09**, `M-held-bytes`, from panel 124 — and read off a red
# run again, which is now twice in one day and the tripwire working as written.
# It is §4.19's FOURTH case, which that section does not name: all three it
# reserved are about a pointer C made, and this is bytes Heroes made and frees
# which C may read for as long as the program says. `REFUSED` gains two more,
# `M-borrowed-pointer` and `M-pinned-bytes`: the first was the coordinator's own
# suggestion in the brief and names §4.19's case 2, which the sitting measured to
# be unreachable from any declaration because `sqlite3.h:4888` puts the decision
# in the fifth ARGUMENT; the second names the one shape panel 124 R3 refuses,
# since a pin aliases `s.cstr()` and a copy-on-write mutation would rewrite what
# C is reading.
# **66 -> 67 on 2026-09-09**, `M-interpolated-strings`: the implementation panel
# 121 R10 said takes an id of its own, because `M-interpolation-verdict` was
# named for a ruling so that it could close with a refusal, and an id may not
# claim what the sitting existed to decide. Named for the deliverable, the
# literal a reader writes, and not for the feature's area.
#
# **67 -> 71 on 2026-09-10**, and it is the first insert of FOUR at once:
# `M-cleanup-verdict`, `M-arm-platform`, `M-deployable-binary` and
# `M-compatibility-promise`, all by author decision out of the session that wrote
# `docs/ROADMAP.md` § What production-ready means — the chain's first reading
# against a criterion, where six of its ten rows turned out to be owned already.
# **This number is moved by hand and not read off a red run**, because the four
# rows and this constant land in one commit; the tripwire's other direction still
# holds, so a fifth row added without touching this line goes red. `REFUSED` gains
# **two** names, both runners-up to `M-arm-platform`: `M-fourth-platform`, refused
# because `M-online-compiler`'s entry already calls a browser the fourth platform
# under panel 114's ruling, so the number names two different things and an id may
# not; and `M-arm-linux`, the narrower spelling, refused because the deliverable is
# a platform measured by `.claude/rules/platforms.md`'s own rules — its three legs
# become four — rather than an operating-system port, and `platform` is the word
# every one of those rules already uses.
#
# **71 -> 72 on 2026-09-10**, `M-microcontroller-verdict`, later the same day and
# from a different question: the author asked whether a Heroes program could run
# on a microcontroller such as the ESP32, and the row is a verdict rather than a
# target, on `M-cleanup-verdict`'s precedent, so that it can close with a
# refusal. Moved by hand again, with the row, in one commit. `REFUSED` gains
# nothing: no runner-up name was written into a living file.
#
# **72 -> 73 on 2026-09-10**, `M-anchored-spec`, the same evening: the author
# found the specification disorganised and decided its re-shaping into thirteen
# numbered sections, so the row enters at 45, before `M-reflection-verdict`,
# because a verdict that adds a sentence to the spec should add it to the
# ordered one. Moved by hand, with the row, in one commit. `REFUSED` gains
# nothing: the runner-up names stayed in the session's plan, outside the tree.
#
# **73 -> 74 on 2026-09-11**, `M-labelled-builtins`, the same evening and out of
# the milestone before it: panel 126's ergonomist found that `fail("a", "b")`
# was accepted where a user function's two `str` parameters are refused, the
# author asked for the repair in one word, and the row is the repair. Moved by
# hand, with the row, in one commit. `REFUSED` gains nothing: the runner-up
# names stayed in the session.
#
# **76 -> 77 on 2026-09-11**, `M-named-callbacks`, scheduled rather than closed:
# the author answered panel 129's second question by asking for the route that
# closes the generic case, over the sitting's own recommendation. `REFUSED` gains
# `M-generic-labels`, the runner-up, refused because the milestone delivers names
# on callbacks and not a generics feature.
#
# **75 -> 76 on 2026-09-11**, `M-labelled-types`, the fourth of the night and
# the one that closed what the third had narrowed: panel 129 judged the route
# panel 128 had priced and scheduled, and three of its five seats sent the work
# back before it landed. Moved by hand, with the row, in one commit. `REFUSED`
# gains `M-named-parameters`, the runner-up, refused because a declaration's
# parameters have been named since v0 and the id would have claimed a topic this
# milestone does not deliver.
#
# **74 -> 75 on 2026-09-11**, `M-positional-values`, the third of the night and
# the third out of the one before it: panel 128 sat on defect 026, vetoed three
# routes and landed the one nobody had listed. Moved by hand, with the row, in
# one commit. `REFUSED` gains `M-function-labels`, the runner-up, refused
# because this milestone delivers the absence of labels at such a call rather
# than their presence.
constant ROWS: i64
    78

# **`WATCHED` stood here until 2026-09-07 and nothing had read it since
# 2026-08-19.** It listed `design.md` and `CLAUDE.md` as *"the two documents whose
# citations are checked"*, and that stopped being true on 2026-09-06, when the
# check began walking the whole tree with no list of exemptions. A grep for the
# name returned exactly one line, its own declaration. It is deleted rather than
# repaired because `records/exemptions` cannot see it: that check reads RECORD,
# GENERATED and QUOTES_THE_OLD_SPELLING, and a fourth list nobody consults reads
# as a rule to whoever finds it next, which is the same failure the exemptions
# check exists to catch one level up.

# The operating contract, whose numbered sections `sections` below resolves. Its
# token ceiling is `suite_spec.hero`'s, where the compiler is in hand to measure
# it; this file only checks that the pointers into it land.
constant CONTRACT: str
    "CLAUDE.md"

# How many tokens one `git check-ignore` carries. `runtime/parts/run.c` panics
# past `HERO_RUN_MAX_ARGS` 256 words, and a token costs two words, so 127 is the
# hard ceiling and this leaves room for the two words the command itself takes
# plus any that grow later. `ignored_by_git` carries the measurement.
constant IGNORE_BATCH: i64
    100

# `/step`'s milestone-close number for § Where we are. A threshold with an
# instrument behind it, which is the only kind that holds.
constant WHERE_CEILING: i64
    15

# **Italian function words, and the reason the list is ASCII only.** CLAUDE.md
# §11's first line says the repository's working text is English. It said so
# while 241 Italian quotations sat in the tree, 19 of them in that very section,
# because a doctrine written at `docs/book/README.md` held that quoted speech is
# not an artifact and everything else cited it. The doctrine is retired
# (2026-09-04) and this is what replaces the honour system.
#
# The words are the ones a byte-oriented tokenizer sees: `latin_words` breaks a
# token at any byte outside `a`–`z`, and every accented character is two bytes
# above 127, so `perché` arrives as `perch` and that is the spelling listed.
# Nothing here is spelled with an accent for the same reason — `così` would
# arrive as `cos`, which is a C function, and `più` as `pi`. A marker that
# collides with English or with mathematics buys a false failure, and a check
# that cries wolf is turned off.
#
# **`un`, `chi`, `nel` and `sia` were added after the list missed a real
# sentence.** *"metti come regola che chi capita sul sito non sia un esperto"* —
# `site/CLAUDE.md`'s own instruction — reached three markers of twelve words and
# stayed under the threshold. With the four it reaches six. They were measured
# before they were added: over every tracked text file outside the records, the
# longer list fires on the same 23 declared files and nothing else.
constant ITALIAN_MARKERS: [str]
    [
        "che"
        "non"
        "sono"
        "questo"
        "questa"
        "quello"
        "quella"
        "della"
        "delle"
        "degli"
        "nella"
        "nello"
        "anche"
        "senza"
        "molto"
        "tutto"
        "tutti"
        "tutte"
        "voglio"
        "cosa"
        "quando"
        "sempre"
        "essere"
        "fare"
        "adesso"
        "ogni"
        "qualcosa"
        "niente"
        "nulla"
        "poi"
        "quindi"
        "mai"
        "solo"
        "una"
        "uno"
        "dei"
        "sul"
        "nel"
        "sia"
        "chi"
        "un"
        "alla"
        "allo"
        "agli"
        "gli"
        "perch"
    ]

# Where Italian is the DELIVERABLE rather than a leak: CLAUDE.md §11's second
# declared exception, a translation somebody reads on purpose. Prefix match, the
# same shape as `is_record`.
#
# **Three entries, and the third arrived on 2026-09-08 exactly as the paragraph
# it replaces predicted.** The Italian edition's page sources are here, and its
# `.astro` wrappers under `site/src/pages/it/` are here now as well. They were
# left out for as long as they could not fire: measured 2026-09-04, **not one of
# the 23 did**, because a wrapper carries one Italian title of five or six words
# and this check needs six words on a line before it looks. `it/404.astro` is
# the first that fires, of 26 wrappers today, because a not-found page owes a
# whole sentence of description where every other page owes a title, and the
# check went red on it before the commit rather than after. `SiteNav.astro`'s
# table of Italian nav labels is still NOT here, on the same measurement and the
# same reasoning. A dead exemption is not free — the same argument that deleted
# `site/log.html` (gone 2026-08-18) from RECORD above — because it reads as a
# licence, and the next person to write a paragraph of Italian into a wrapper
# would believe it had one. A live one is the record of a deliverable, which is
# what §11's second exception exists for.
constant ITALIAN_BY_DESIGN: [str]
    [
        "site/src/html/it"
        "site/src/pages/it"
        ".claude/skills/where/italian-vocabulary.md"
    ]

# The record that lives under no record DIRECTORY. `docs/work/DONE.md` is
# append-only and never rewritten (CLAUDE.md §3 and §14), so it keeps the words
# it was written with exactly as `docs/panel/` does — about 38 Italian quotations
# among them, left there on purpose. It is missing from `RECORD` only because
# `quoting` already takes it out of the milestone walk for an unrelated reason.
#
# **This is why `english` does not walk `living`.** `living` also drops
# `CLAUDE.md` and `docs/ROADMAP.md` — they quote the retired milestone spelling,
# so `quoting` removes them — and those two held **35 of the 55 Italian spans**
# repaired on 2026-09-04. A check reading `living` would have run green over the
# exact two files it exists for. It was written that way first, and caught
# before the commit by asking which files the walk actually hands it.
constant APPENDED: [str]
    [
        "docs/work/DONE.md"
    ]

# **The same words again, and many more of them, for use INSIDE A QUOTATION
# ONLY.** The list above is what a line of mixed English prose can be scanned
# with; this is what a span already known to be a quotation can be scanned with,
# and the difference is the whole reason there are two.
#
# **The line rule alone caught 10 of 42.** Measured 2026-09-04 by replaying the
# 42 Italian spans this repository actually held, out of `d7c2661b`, against the
# check as first written: it found the Italian *pages*, whose paragraphs are
# Italian end to end, and it walked straight past the shape this repository is
# full of — six Italian words quoted inside an English sentence. `*"convoca il
# panel e finisci"*` is five words on a line of thirty, and no per-line density
# will ever see it. The span rule reads the quotation on its own terms and
# catches **41 of the 42**.
#
# **Why the extra words cannot be used on a line.** `i`, `e`, `di` and `per` are
# what make a five-word quotation legible, and they are also what a `.hero`
# signature is made of: with this list on the line rule,
# `function step(@m: Machine, opcode: i64, a: i64, b: i64) -> i64?` reads as
# Italian, and so do 46 other lines of real code and comments — measured, the
# same session. Inside `*"…"*` there is no code to trip over, so the same words
# stop being noise and become evidence.
constant QUOTED_MARKERS: [str]
    [
        "il"
        "la"
        "le"
        "lo"
        "gli"
        "i"
        "un"
        "una"
        "uno"
        "del"
        "della"
        "delle"
        "dei"
        "degli"
        "nel"
        "nella"
        "nello"
        "sul"
        "sulla"
        "alla"
        "allo"
        "agli"
        "con"
        "che"
        "non"
        "mi"
        "ti"
        "ci"
        "si"
        "se"
        "ma"
        "poi"
        "sempre"
        "mai"
        "anche"
        "senza"
        "tutto"
        "tutti"
        "tutte"
        "voglio"
        "fai"
        "fare"
        "cosa"
        "quando"
        "dove"
        "solo"
        "sono"
        "essere"
        "questo"
        "questa"
        "quello"
        "quella"
        "chi"
        "sia"
        "perch"
        "quindi"
        "niente"
        "nulla"
        "molto"
        "ogni"
        "adesso"
        "qualcosa"
        "piu"
        "per"
        "di"
        "e"
    ]

# **The one file that must hold Italian in order to refuse it: this one.** Its
# test quotes the sentences the check has to catch, and a check that cannot
# state its own witnesses is a check nobody can read or trust. It is the same
# argument, in the same module, as `QUOTES_THE_OLD_SPELLING` above, which is
# there because these assertions quote `M0` and `M5b`.
#
# Exact match and one entry, so the exemption cannot spread: any OTHER harness
# file that grows an Italian line is reported like anything else.
constant QUOTES_THE_ITALIAN: [str]
    [
        "tests/harness/suite_records.hero"
    ]

function run_all(scratch: str) -> report.Report?
    r: report.Report @ report.empty()

    # The quoting files leave the walk ONCE, for every check — which is
    # what the Rust `walk` does and what the first draft of this port got
    # wrong by filtering inside one check only. The three deliberate
    # misspellings `milestones.rs` quotes (to say what this check catches)
    # were reported as three real defects.
    # **Git answers which files are this project's** (shell.project_files), and
    # RECORD then removes the ones that are RECORDS. The two questions used to be
    # one `find` with a list of exclusions, and that list could not keep up with
    # the world: a gitignored TLS key in `site/.cache/` was read as `M3KN` on
    # 2026-08-19 — a milestone identifier, in a file nobody wrote and git already
    # knew to ignore.
    walked = shell.project_files(scratch)?
    living: [str] @ []

    for path in walked
        if !quoting(path) && !is_record(path) && !is_generated(path)
            living @ living.push(path)

    numbered(@r, living: living)
    named(@r, living: living)
    sections(@r, living: living)
    c_files(@r, living: living)
    citations(@r, scratch: scratch, present: walked)
    english(@r, present: walked)
    exemptions(@r, present: walked)
    rules(@r, present: walked)
    expectations(@r, scratch: scratch)
    boundary(@r)
    verdicts(@r, present: walked)
    where_we_are(@r)
    homed(@r, present: walked)
    lists(@r, present: walked)
    tagged(@r, scratch: scratch)
    appended(@r, scratch: scratch)
    numbering(@r, present: walked)
    unmerged(@r, scratch: scratch)
    rotated(@r, present: walked)
    positions(@r, scratch: scratch, present: walked)
    return ok(r)

# A MILESTONE IS TAGGED ONLY OVER A CLEAN LIST (author instruction
# 2026-09-08, and CLAUDE.md § Verification is the rule's home).
#
# The author asked for zero open decisions and zero open defects at a tag.
# Zero defects is taken whole: an open defect is something BROKEN, and
# nothing depends on it staying open. Zero decisions is taken with one
# exception, named rather than assumed, because the literal rule would
# block every milestone that held a sitting: § 4 says a panel never
# blocks, so each one leaves a `panel NNN` item, and
# `records/verdicts` above catches an unratified sitting only while that
# item exists. A ratification pending is waiting on the AUTHOR, not
# broken.
#
# IT READS THE NEWEST TAG'S OWN COMMIT and not the working tree, which is
# what makes the rule livable: a defect filed at 10am and repaired at 4pm
# is ordinary work, and only a tag placed over it is the mistake. So this
# check is silent all through a milestone and speaks once, at its close.
#
# It binds from the tag it first sees onwards. Older tags are not
# re-judged, which is not leniency but § 14: a record is not rewritten,
# and a rule cannot be applied to a commit made before it existed.
function tagged(@r: report.Report, scratch: str)
    listed = shell.run(
        scratch: scratch,
        program: "git",
        words: ["git", "tag", "--list", "m-*", "--sort=-creatordate"]
    )

    if listed.is_err()
        report.keep_failure(@r, label: "records/tagged", detail: "  cannot list the milestone tags")
        return
    newest: str @ ""

    for row in strings.lines(listed.must().out)
        if newest == "" && strings.trimmed(row) != ""
            newest @ strings.trimmed(row)

    if newest == ""
        # No milestone has been tagged yet, which is a true state of this
        # repository's first day and not a failure.
        report.keep_pass(@r)
        return
    open_defects = open_items_at(scratch: scratch, tag: newest, path: "docs/work/DEFECTS.md")

    if open_defects.is_err()
        report.keep_failure(@r, label: "records/tagged", detail: "  cannot read DEFECTS.md at " + newest)
        return

    if open_defects.must().len() > 0
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "records/tagged",
            detail: "  " + newest + " was placed over " + open_defects.must().len().to_str() + " open defect(s):\n    " + open_defects.must().join("\n    ") + "\n  A milestone is tagged only over a clean list (CLAUDE.md § Verification).\n  An open defect is something BROKEN, and a tag over one says the opposite."
        )
        return
    open_decisions = open_items_at(scratch: scratch, tag: newest, path: "docs/work/DECIDE.md")

    if open_decisions.is_err()
        report.keep_failure(@r, label: "records/tagged", detail: "  cannot read DECIDE.md at " + newest)
        return
    strict = carried_the_rule(scratch: scratch, tag: newest)

    if strict.is_err()
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "records/tagged",
            detail: "  cannot read " + EXECUTOR + " at " + newest + "\n  Without it this check cannot tell whether that tag was placed under the\n  strict reading of a ratification or the historical one, and reading the\n  silence as either would weaken this check without saying so."
        )
        return
    unqueued: [str] @ []

    for row in open_decisions.must()
        held: bool @ strings.contains(text: row, needle: "panel ")

        if strict.must()
            held @ is_ratification(row: row)

        if !held
            unqueued @ unqueued.push(row)

    if unqueued.len() > 0
        reading: str @ "the historical reading, the substring `panel `, because that tag's own\n  tree did not carry the strict one"

        if strict.must()
            reading @ "the strict reading: the origin field is `panel NNN` and nothing more, so\n  a sitting's FINDING does not count as its ratification"
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "records/tagged",
            detail: "  " + newest + " was placed over " + unqueued.len().to_str() + " open decision(s) that are not a sitting's ratification:\n    " + unqueued.join("\n    ") + "\n  A pending `panel NNN` is allowed to stand at a tag, because § 4 says the\n  panel never blocks. Anything else is a decision this milestone could have\n  settled and left hanging instead (CLAUDE.md § Verification).\n  Applied: " + reading + "."
        )
        return
    report.keep_pass(@r)

# The `- [ ] ` lines of a list AS IT WAS at a tag, which is the only way to
# ask what a tag was placed over. `git show <tag>:<path>` and not the
# working tree, deliberately: see `tagged`.
function open_items_at(scratch: str, tag: str, path: str) -> [str]?
    shown = shell.run(scratch: scratch, program: "git", words: ["git", "show", tag + ":" + path])?

    if shown.code != 0
        return fail(code: "no_such_file", msg: path + " does not exist at " + tag)
    open_rows: [str] @ []

    for row in strings.lines(shown.out)
        if strings.starts_with(text: row, prefix: "- [ ] ")
            open_rows @ open_rows.push(strings.trimmed(row))

    return ok(open_rows)

# THE STRICT READING OF *a sitting's ratification*, AND WHY IT ASKS THE TAG'S
# OWN TREE WHETHER IT CARRIED THIS RULE.
#
# `tagged` accepted any row containing the substring `panel `, which accepts
# `- [ ] **panel 118 finding**` as though it were a ratification. It is not
# one. A pending `panel NNN` is waiting on the AUTHOR, which is why § 4 lets it
# stand at a tag; a FINDING is a decision the milestone could have settled and
# left hanging, which is the exact thing this check exists to refuse. The
# measurement that settles it rather than an opinion: `m-closures-verdict` was
# placed over three `panel 118 finding` items on 2026-09-08 and **all three
# were settled by one `/decide` session** the same day, two of them without
# needing the author at all — one was already answered by design.md §1.12
# before it was asked. Found by reading this function while zeroing the list;
# the author asked for the repair the same day.
#
# So the rule reads the ORIGIN FIELD — `**panel NNN**` and nothing more.
#
# **AND IT BINDS ONLY ON A TAG WHOSE OWN TREE CARRIED IT**, which is this
# neighbourhood's own comment turned into code instead of left as a sentence:
# *a rule cannot be applied to a commit made before it existed*, and
# `m-closures-verdict` was placed under the loose reading, and a record is not
# rewritten (CLAUDE.md §14). The alternative was a constant naming that tag,
# and this file already carries a check — `exemptions` — that exists because a
# dead entry sat for weeks reading like a live rule. Asking the tree needs no
# such constant and retires itself.
#
# The three outcomes are kept apart deliberately: the marker present is the
# strict reading, the marker absent is the historical one, and the executor
# **unreadable** at that tag is a reported failure. Collapsing the third into
# the second is what would make a rename weaken this check in silence, which is
# the one direction this repository refuses.
constant EXECUTOR: str
    "tests/harness/suite_records.hero"

# The sentence a tag's tree must carry for the strict reading to bind. A
# CONSTANT for `VERDICT_HEADING`'s reason, and its value reaches the tree by
# being written here, which is the whole mechanism: this file contains it
# because this line is in it.
constant RATIFICATION_MARKER: str
    "tagged reads the origin field and not the substring"

# Whether `newest`'s own tree carried the strict rule. An error means the
# executor could not be read there and is never read as either answer.
function carried_the_rule(scratch: str, tag: str) -> bool?
    shown = shell.run(scratch: scratch, program: "git", words: ["git", "show", tag + ":" + EXECUTOR])?

    if shown.code != 0
        return fail(code: "no_such_file", msg: EXECUTOR + " does not exist at " + tag)

    return ok(strings.contains(text: shown.out, needle: RATIFICATION_MARKER))

# A ratification row's origin field is `panel NNN` and nothing else. The
# padded spelling is `docs/work/DECIDE.md`'s own rule, written there for
# `queued`'s sake, so this reads what that file already promises rather than
# inventing a second shape.
function is_ratification(row: str) -> bool
    at = strings.index_of(text: row, needle: "**")

    if at.is_err()
        return false
    rest = row.slice(from: at.must() + 2, to: row.len())
    close = strings.index_of(text: rest, needle: "**")

    if close.is_err()
        return false
    field = rest.slice(from: 0, to: close.must())

    if !strings.starts_with(text: field, prefix: "panel ")
        return false
    number = strings.to_number(text: strings.without_prefix(text: field, prefix: "panel "))

    # `to_number` reads a leading `-`, so the sign is refused here rather than
    # left to a spelling nobody writes today (`.claude/rules/module-shape.md`:
    # close the class, not the reachable case).
    return !number.is_err() && number.must() > 0

test "a ratification row is `panel NNN` and a finding row is not"
    assert is_ratification(row: "- [ ] **panel 120** | ratify or overturn | `docs/panel/120`")
    assert is_ratification(row: "- [ ] **panel 7** | one digit is a sitting too | x")

    # The row this whole repair exists for. It contains `panel ` and it is not
    # a ratification, which is what the substring test could not tell.
    assert !is_ratification(row: "- [ ] **panel 118 finding** | may a `certain` fix preserve the bug? | x")
    assert strings.contains(text: "- [ ] **panel 118 finding** | x", needle: "panel ")

test "the shapes beside a ratification row, one at a time"
    # No origin field at all, an unclosed one, an empty one, a non-sitting
    # origin, a sitting with no number, and a number that is not one.
    assert !is_ratification(row: "- [ ] panel 120 | no bold at all | x")
    assert !is_ratification(row: "- [ ] **panel 120 | unclosed | x")
    assert !is_ratification(row: "- [ ] **** | empty | x")
    assert !is_ratification(row: "- [ ] **M-core-packages** | a milestone, not a sitting | x")
    assert !is_ratification(row: "- [ ] **panel ** | no number | x")
    assert !is_ratification(row: "- [ ] **panel twelve** | a word, not a number | x")
    assert !is_ratification(row: "- [ ] **panel -5** | a sign is not a sitting | x")
    assert !is_ratification(row: "- [ ] **panel 118 R5** | a resolution, not a sitting | x")

# Whether this path is one of the dated records — the pruning `find` used to do
# with `-not -path`, done here now that the listing comes from git. Same rule as
# the Rust twin's `rel == *r || rel.starts_with(&format!("{r}/"))`.
function is_record(path: str) -> bool
    for entry in RECORD
        if path == entry || strings.starts_with(text: path, prefix: entry + "/")
            return true

    return false

# Whether this path is an artifact nobody writes. Exact match, and the reason
# it is not a prefix match like `is_record` is up at GENERATED.
function is_generated(path: str) -> bool
    for entry in GENERATED
        if path == entry
            return true

    return false

# Every exemption still selects a file.
#
# This is the check `site/log.html` (deleted 2026-08-18) needed and did not have. That entry stopped
# matching anything the day the site's pages moved into `site/public/`, and it
# went on sitting in RECORD reading like a rule — the walk skipped nothing, and
# nothing said so. The comment above QUOTES_THE_OLD_SPELLING describes the same
# accident from the other side: a record was renamed, its SUFFIX match silently
# stopped firing, and 486 KB of legacy identifiers would have been reported as
# breaches. Both are one failure, an exemption that no longer reaches anything,
# and it is invisible in both directions — the list still reads as correct.
#
# The three lists match differently and the question is the same for all three:
# does this line still reach a file git tracks? RECORD names trees, GENERATED
# names one file exactly, QUOTES_THE_OLD_SPELLING matches a suffix.
function exemptions(@r: report.Report, present: [str])
    dead: [str] @ []

    for entry in RECORD
        if !selects_tree(entry: entry, present: present)
            dead @ dead.push("    RECORD: " + entry)

    for entry in GENERATED
        if !selects_exactly(entry: entry, present: present)
            dead @ dead.push("    GENERATED: " + entry)

    for entry in QUOTES_THE_OLD_SPELLING
        if !selects_suffix(entry: entry, present: present)
            dead @ dead.push("    QUOTES_THE_OLD_SPELLING: " + entry)

    # THE OTHER ELEVEN LISTS, none of which was covered until 2026-09-12. Three
    # were checked and eleven were not, which is the same asymmetry this function
    # exists to close one level down: a list nobody asks about goes on reading
    # like a rule long after it stops selecting anything.
    for entry in HOMES
        if entry != "" && !selects_tree(entry: strings.without_suffix(text: entry, suffix: "/"), present: present)
            dead @ dead.push("    HOMES: " + entry)

    for entry in APPENDED
        if !selects_exactly(entry: entry, present: present)
            dead @ dead.push("    APPENDED: " + entry)

    for entry in [DECISIONS, DEFECTS, LEARNING, RECORD_LIST, MILESTONES]
        if !selects_tree(entry: entry, present: present)
            dead @ dead.push("    a named list: " + entry)

    for entry in DEFECT_NUMBERED
        if !selects_tree(entry: entry, present: present)
            dead @ dead.push("    DEFECT_NUMBERED: " + entry)

    for entry in APPEND_ONLY
        if !selects_exactly(entry: strings.words(entry)[0], present: present)
            dead @ dead.push("    APPEND_ONLY: " + entry)

    for entry in ROTATED
        fields = strings.words(entry)

        if fields.len() == 3
            if !selects_exactly(entry: fields[0], present: present)
                dead @ dead.push("    ROTATED, the map: " + fields[0])

            if !selects_tree(entry: fields[2], present: present)
                dead @ dead.push("    ROTATED, the tree: " + fields[2])

    # AND THE INVERSE RULE, which is the only one of its kind here: a retired
    # root must select NOTHING. A root that comes back is not retired, and the
    # entry saying so would be a lie nobody reads twice.
    for entry in RETIRED_ROOTS
        if selects_tree(entry: entry, present: present)
            dead @ dead.push("    RETIRED_ROOTS, and it is back: " + entry)

    if dead.len() > 0
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "records/exemptions",
            detail: "  an exemption selects no file this repository tracks:\n" + dead.join("\n") + "\n  Either the path moved, and the entry has to follow it, or nothing needs the\n  exemption any more and the line comes out. A rule that reaches nothing still\n  reads as a rule to whoever finds it next."
        )
    else
        report.keep_pass(@r)

function selects_tree(entry: str, present: [str]) -> bool
    for path in present
        if path == entry || strings.starts_with(text: path, prefix: entry + "/")
            return true

    return false

function selects_exactly(entry: str, present: [str]) -> bool
    for path in present
        if path == entry
            return true

    return false

function selects_suffix(entry: str, present: [str]) -> bool
    for path in present
        if strings.ends_with(text: path, suffix: entry)
            return true

    return false

# EVERY `paths:` GLOB OF A TREE-LOCAL RULE REACHES SOMETHING.
#
# `.claude/rules/*.md` load on a path match and cost nothing in the sessions that
# never go there (CLAUDE.md § Where the rest lives). That is the whole design,
# and it has one failure mode nobody could see: **nothing verifies that a rule
# still loads**. A directory moves, its glob stops matching, and the rule simply
# never arrives — no error, no red, no session the wiser. It is `exemptions`
# one level up, about the rules instead of the lists.
#
# The matcher is the prefix up to the first `*`, which is enough for every shape
# these files use: `docs/**` asks for `docs`, `selfhost/emit/ffi*` asks for
# `selfhost/emit/ffi` and is a partial FILE name, so the test is a plain prefix
# and not a tree.
function rules(@r: report.Report, present: [str])
    listed = shell.run(
        scratch: ".",
        program: "git",
        words: ["git", "ls-files", ".claude/rules"]
    )

    if listed.is_err()
        report.keep_failure(@r, label: "records/rules", detail: "  cannot list `.claude/rules`")
        return
    dead: [str] @ []
    globs: i64 @ 0

    for rule in strings.lines(listed.must().out)
        if !strings.ends_with(text: rule, suffix: ".md")
            continue
        text = read_file(strings.trimmed(rule))

        if text.is_err()
            dead @ dead.push("    cannot read " + rule)
            continue
        fences: i64 @ 0

        for row in strings.lines(text.must())
            if strings.trimmed(row) == "---"
                fences @ fences + 1

            if fences != 1
                continue

            if !strings.starts_with(text: strings.trimmed(row), prefix: "- ")
                continue
            glob = unquoted(strings.without_prefix(text: strings.trimmed(row), prefix: "- "))
            globs @ globs + 1

            if !selects_prefix(entry: prefix_of(glob), present: present)
                dead @ dead.push("    " + rule + ": `" + glob + "` reaches nothing")

    if globs == 0
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "records/rules",
            detail: "  no `paths:` glob was read at all, so this check would pass however the\n  rules were pointed. Either `.claude/rules` moved, or the frontmatter stopped\n  being the shape this reader knows."
        )
        return

    if dead.len() > 0
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "records/rules",
            detail: "  a tree-local rule points at nothing, so it never loads:\n" + dead.join("\n") + "\n  A rule that cannot be reached still reads as a rule to whoever finds it, and\n  nothing else in this repository would have said so. Repoint the glob, or take\n  the line out because nothing needs the rule any more."
        )
    else
        report.keep_pass(@r)

# The part of a glob before its first `*`: `docs/**` gives `docs`, and
# `selfhost/emit/ffi*` gives `selfhost/emit/ffi`.
function prefix_of(glob: str) -> str
    at = strings.index_of(text: glob, needle: "*")

    if at.is_err()
        return glob
    cut = glob.slice(from: 0, to: at.must())

    if strings.ends_with(text: cut, suffix: "/")
        return cut.slice(from: 0, to: cut.len() - 1)

    return cut

function unquoted(text: str) -> str
    out: str @ text

    if strings.starts_with(text: out, prefix: "\"")
        out @ out.slice(from: 1, to: out.len())

    if strings.ends_with(text: out, suffix: "\"")
        out @ out.slice(from: 0, to: out.len() - 1)

    return out

function selects_prefix(entry: str, present: [str]) -> bool
    for path in present
        if path == entry || strings.starts_with(text: path, prefix: entry)
            return true

    return false

# THE APPEND-ONLY RULE HAD NO EXECUTOR UNTIL THIS BLOCK, AND THAT IS MEASURED
# RATHER THAN SUSPECTED.
#
# CLAUDE.md §14 says a record is never rewritten: a falsified sentence is
# corrected underneath, with its date, never deleted. Four documents state it —
# the contract, `.claude/rules/records.md`, this file's own RECORD comment, and
# `docs/work/DONE.md`'s preamble — and until 2026-09-12 nothing performed it.
# Commit `76799a18` removed one `- [x]` line from the record and every suite
# stayed green. A rule stated everywhere and executed nowhere is CL-031's story,
# told here about the rule those four documents exist to protect.
#
# THE WINDOW IS THE WORKING TREE, NOT THE LAST COMMIT, and the choice is
# deliberate twice over. It speaks while the deletion is still yours to undo,
# which is when a warning is worth anything; and `git diff HEAD` needs no
# history, so it reads the same on this Mac and in a CI leg whose checkout is
# `fetch-depth: 1` and has no `HEAD~1` to compare against. A check that could
# only run where the history is deep would be a check that never ran in CI.
#
# The number is the record's FIRST LINE, not its banner's: two of the three
# records have no banner at all. Above it is the preamble, which is prose
# somebody may correct — `7c2198ff` rewrote eighteen lines of DONE's and was
# right to. Below it is the record.
constant APPEND_ONLY: [str]
    [
        "docs/contract/case-law.md 34"
    ]

function appended(@r: report.Report, scratch: str)
    offences: [str] @ []

    if APPEND_ONLY.len() == 0
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "records/appended",
            detail: "  this list is empty, so this check guards nothing and passes for the\n  wrong reason. Every record that grows BY LINE belongs here; one that\n  grows by FILE belongs in ROTATED, where its height is pinned instead."
        )
        return

    for entry in APPEND_ONLY
        fields = strings.words(entry)

        if fields.len() != 2
            offences @ offences.push("    " + entry + " is not `<path> <first record line>`")
            continue
        floor = strings.to_number(fields[1]).default(0)

        if floor == 0
            offences @ offences.push("    " + entry + " does not name a line number")
            continue
        diffed = shell.run(
            scratch: scratch,
            program: "git",
            words: ["git", "diff", "HEAD", "--", fields[0]]
        )

        if diffed.is_err()
            offences @ offences.push("    cannot ask git what the working tree changed in " + fields[0])
            continue

        for row in removed_below(text: diffed.must().out, floor: floor)
            offences @ offences.push("    " + fields[0] + ": " + row)

    if offences.len() > 0
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "records/appended",
            detail: "  a line is being removed from a record:\n" + offences.join("\n") + "\n  A record is never rewritten (CLAUDE.md §14). Where the sentence has since\n  been falsified, the correction is ADDED UNDERNEATH with its date: a\n  measurement that was right when it was taken is history, and a record that\n  quietly loses its inconvenient half is worth less than none.\n  The preamble above the record's first line is not covered and may be edited."
        )
    else
        report.keep_pass(@r)

# The lines a working-tree diff removes at or below `floor`, read out of the
# hunk headers rather than out of the file. A unified diff carries its own
# numbering — `@@ -240,7 +240,9 @@` says the next old line is 240 — so where a
# removal lands is readable without opening anything.
#
# Nothing before the first `@@` is counted: `--- a/x` and `+++ b/x` open with
# the same bytes as a removal and an addition, and reading them as content is
# how a diff parser reports the file header as a deleted line.
function removed_below(text: str, floor: i64) -> [str]
    out: [str] @ []
    old: i64 @ 0
    started: bool @ false

    for row in strings.lines(text)
        if strings.starts_with(text: row, prefix: "@@ -")
            old @ hunk_start(row)
            started @ true
        else if started
            if strings.starts_with(text: row, prefix: "-")
                if old >= floor
                    out @ out.push(strings.trimmed(row))
                old @ old + 1
            else if !strings.starts_with(text: row, prefix: "+")
                old @ old + 1

    return out

# The old-file line a hunk opens at: 240, out of `@@ -240,7 +240,9 @@`. A
# header this cannot read yields 0, which `removed_below` then compares against
# a floor of at least 1 — so an unreadable header reports every removal in that
# hunk rather than none. The loud direction (`.claude/rules/module-shape.md`).
function hunk_start(row: str) -> i64
    rest = strings.without_prefix(text: row, prefix: "@@ -")
    end: i64 @ 0

    while end < rest.len()
        byte = rest[end]

        if byte < '0' || byte > '9'
            break
        end @ end + 1

    if end == 0
        return 0

    return strings.to_number(rest.slice(from: 0, to: end)).default(0)

# NO DEFECT NUMBER IS ISSUED TWICE.
#
# The preamble of `docs/work/DEFECTS.md` says *the next number to issue is NNN*,
# and that sentence is shared state: two sessions reading the same trunk both
# read the same number, both take it, and the merge is CLEAN AND WRONG — two
# defects carrying one identifier, forever, in a record nobody may rewrite. It
# has already happened once without any parallel work at all, which is why the
# preamble itself records that 014 was issued twice.
#
# So the rule stops being a sentence and becomes a reading: the next number is
# one above the highest this check finds.
constant DEFECT_NUMBERED: [str]
    [
        "docs/work/DEFECTS.md"
        "docs/done"
    ]

# The one number this record carries twice, named and dated instead of letting
# the rule be loosened to accommodate it. It is in the record and the record is
# not rewritten, so the exception is exact and it retires itself the day the
# record it names stops existing (the shape of `VERDICT_FLOOR`).
constant ISSUED_TWICE: str
    "014"

# A ROTATED RECORD IS A TREE, AND THIS CHECK WALKS IT. An entry of
# `DEFECT_NUMBERED` names either a file or the root of one, and the difference
# is resolved against what git tracks rather than against the disk — the same
# oracle `project_files` is. This is the line the rotation of `docs/work/DONE.md`
# demanded: the moment the record became a map, the check read zero numbers and
# **said so** instead of passing, which is what its floor is for.
function readable(entry: str, present: [str]) -> [str]
    for path in present
        if path == entry
            return [entry]

    under: [str] @ []

    for path in present
        if strings.starts_with(text: path, prefix: entry + "/")
            under @ under.push(path)

    return under

function numbering(@r: report.Report, present: [str])
    rows: [str] @ []
    sources: [str] @ []

    for entry in DEFECT_NUMBERED
        for path in readable(entry: entry, present: present)
            sources @ sources.push(path)

    for path in sources
        text = read_file(path)

        if text.is_err()
            report.keep_failure(
                @r,
                label: "records/numbering",
                detail: "  cannot read " + path + ", and this check cannot tell whether a number\n  is issued twice without reading every file that issues one."
            )
            return

        for row in strings.lines(text.must())
            rows @ rows.push(row)

    issued = numbers_issued(rows)

    if issued.len() == 0
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "records/numbering",
            detail: "  this check read zero defect numbers, so it would pass whatever the\n  record held. Either the entry shape moved and this reader did not follow\n  it, or the files it walks are not the files that issue numbers."
        )
        return
    twice = issued_twice(issued)

    if twice.len() > 0
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "records/numbering",
            detail: "  a defect number is issued twice:\n    " + twice.join("\n    ") + "\n  The next number to issue is one above the highest here, read rather than\n  remembered: a sentence in a preamble is shared state, and two sessions\n  reading the same trunk take the same number and merge cleanly."
        )
    else
        report.keep_pass(@r)

# Every defect number these rows issue, in the order they issue it, with the
# one the record carries twice left out. Split from the check so the shape can
# be driven by literals in a test, which is `list_offences`'s division and for
# `list_offences`'s reason: a reader of the check sees what it decides, and a
# reader of the test sees every shape it has decided about.
function numbers_issued(rows: [str]) -> [str]
    out: [str] @ []

    for row in rows
        number = defect_number(row)

        if number != "" && number != ISSUED_TWICE
            out @ out.push(number)

    return out

function issued_twice(numbers: [str]) -> [str]
    seen: [str] @ []
    twice: [str] @ []

    for number in numbers
        if is_among(number, seen: seen) && !is_among(number, seen: twice)
            twice @ twice.push(number)
        seen @ seen.push(number)

    return twice

# The `NNN` of `- [ ] **NNN — title**`, or the empty string where the line is
# not a defect item. It asks the value in hand — this row's own bytes — and
# never how many defects the project is supposed to have.
function defect_number(row: str) -> str
    marker: str @ ""

    if strings.starts_with(text: row, prefix: "- [ ] **")
        marker @ "- [ ] **"

    if strings.starts_with(text: row, prefix: "- [x] **")
        marker @ "- [x] **"

    if marker == ""
        return ""
    rest = strings.without_prefix(text: row, prefix: marker)

    if rest.len() < 4
        return ""
    i: i64 @ 0

    while i < 3
        byte = rest[i]

        if byte < '0' || byte > '9'
            return ""
        i @ i + 1

    number = rest.slice(from: 0, to: 3)

    if !strings.starts_with(text: strings.without_prefix(text: rest, prefix: number), prefix: " — ")
        return ""

    return number

function is_among(needle: str, seen: [str]) -> bool
    for one in seen
        if one == needle
            return true

    return false

# NO TRACKED FILE CARRIES A MERGE CONFLICT MARKER.
#
# This is the way to fail that working in parallel introduces, and the one no
# other check here can see: a resolution left half-finished enters a commit and
# stays, and every reader in this file — the citation walk, the English walk,
# the list reader — takes the marker for ordinary prose and carries on. It is
# loud the moment somebody looks and invisible until then.
#
# GIT IS ASKED RATHER THAN THE FILES. `git grep` reads the index in one
# process, over exactly the paths git tracks, which is the same oracle
# `project_files` uses and a fraction of the cost of reading every file again.
#
# THE PATTERN IS BUILT AND NOT WRITTEN, and that is not a flourish: an
# instrument that looked for a marker by spelling one out would report ITSELF
# on its first run, and the lesson a reader would take from that is that this
# check cannot be trusted.
function unmerged(@r: report.Report, scratch: str)
    opening = repeat("<", 7)
    closing = repeat(">", 7)
    found = shell.run(
        scratch: scratch,
        program: "git",
        words: ["git", "grep", "-l", "-E", "^(" + opening + "|" + closing + ") "]
    )

    if found.is_err()
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "records/unmerged",
            detail: "  cannot ask git whether a tracked file carries a conflict marker"
        )
        return
    run = found.must()

    if run.code == 1
        # git grep exits 1 when it matched nothing, which is the ordinary state
        # of this repository and the only quiet outcome here.
        report.keep_pass(@r)
        return

    if run.code != 0
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "records/unmerged",
            detail: "  git grep exited " + run.code.to_str() + ", so nothing was searched:\n    " + strings.trimmed(run.err)
        )
        return
    files: [str] @ []

    for row in strings.lines(run.out)
        if strings.trimmed(row) != ""
            files @ files.push("    " + strings.trimmed(row))

    report.keep_failure(
        @r,
        label: "records/unmerged",
        detail: "  a tracked file carries an unresolved merge conflict:\n" + files.join("\n") + "\n  Every other check here reads the marker as prose and passes. Resolve the\n  conflict in the file, then run this suite again."
    )

# A ROTATED RECORD: the entries live one per file in a tree, and the file that
# held them stays behind as a MAP with the line count it had.
#
# The map is what makes the rotation free of damage, and the damage it avoids is
# the invisible kind. Measured 2026-09-12: this repository cites
# `DESIGN-LOG.md:NNN` and `DESIGN-LOG:NNN` **133** times, `DONE.md:NNN` 28 times
# and `ROADMAP.md:NNN` 19, and `.claude/rules/spec-shape.md` states the rule that
# makes those dangerous — CL-037, *a line number is the one citation shape no
# instrument can see*. Moving an entry out of a record would leave every one of
# them pointing at whatever slid into its place, with the whole net green.
#
# So the entry moves and its LINE does not. A citation resolves to the line it
# always named, and that line now says which file holds what it named. Better
# than before, and this is the half worth having: the map can be CHECKED, which
# is `positions` below, so 161 blind citations become 161 verified ones.
#
# Each row is `<map> <lines> <tree>`, the shape `DECIDED` uses in
# `tests/harness/suite_layout.hero`. The line count is pinned rather than
# derived: a map that grows is a map somebody appended to, and the entry they
# appended is the one no citation can reach.
constant ROTATED: [str]
    [
        "docs/book/beats.md 140 docs/book/beats"
        "DESIGN-LOG.md 1119 docs/log"
        "docs/work/DONE.md 4071 docs/done"
    ]

function rotated(@r: report.Report, present: [str])
    offences: [str] @ []

    for entry in ROTATED
        fields = strings.words(entry)

        if fields.len() != 3
            offences @ offences.push("    " + entry + " is not `<map> <lines> <tree>`")
            continue
        map_path = fields[0]
        pinned = strings.to_number(fields[1]).default(0)
        tree = fields[2]
        text = read_file(map_path)

        if text.is_err()
            offences @ offences.push("    cannot read the map " + map_path)
            continue
        rows = strings.lines(text.must())

        if rows.len() != pinned
            offences @ offences.push(
                "    " + map_path + " is " + rows.len().to_str() + " lines and was rotated at " + pinned.to_str() + ": a citation by line no longer lands where it did"
            )

        for row in rows
            points_at = tree_path_in(row: row, tree: tree)

            if points_at != "" && !is_among(points_at, seen: present)
                offences @ offences.push("    " + map_path + " names " + points_at + ", which git does not track")

        held: i64 @ 0

        for path in present
            if strings.starts_with(text: path, prefix: tree + "/")
                held @ held + 1

                if !is_entry_name(strings.base_name(path))
                    offences @ offences.push(
                        "    " + path + " is not named `YYYY-MM-DD-HHMM-<slug>.md`, and the directory is read in name order"
                    )

        if held == 0
            offences @ offences.push(
                "    " + tree + " holds nothing, so this check read zero entries and would pass whatever the record had lost"
            )

    if offences.len() > 0
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "records/rotated",
            detail: "  a rotated record and its tree disagree:\n" + offences.join("\n") + "\n  The map keeps the line count the record had, so that every `<file>:NNN`\n  citation resolves to the entry it named (CL-037: a line number is the one\n  citation shape no instrument can see). A new entry is a NEW FILE in the\n  tree, never a line appended here."
        )
    else
        report.keep_pass(@r)

# The path a map row hands on, or the empty string.
#
# A PATH HAS NO SPACE IN IT, and that sentence is the whole function. The first
# draft took everything from the tree's name to the first `.md`, and the map's
# own preamble then read as a path: it names the tree, says `docs/log/`, and
# mentions `design.md` nine words later, so the two were sewn into one name that
# git does not track and the check reported the preamble as a missing entry. It
# was right to fire and wrong about what it had found. So the name ends where a
# name ends — at a space, a backtick or the end of the line — and only then is
# it asked whether it is markdown.
function tree_path_in(row: str, tree: str) -> str
    at = strings.index_of(text: row, needle: tree + "/")

    if at.is_err()
        return ""
    tail = row.slice(from: at.must(), to: row.len())
    end: i64 @ 0

    while end < tail.len()
        byte = tail[end]

        if byte == ' ' || byte == '`' || byte == '\t'
            break
        end @ end + 1

    name = tail.slice(from: 0, to: end)

    if !strings.ends_with(text: name, suffix: ".md")
        return ""

    return name

# `YYYY-MM-DD-HHMM-<slug>.md`, and nothing else, because the directory is read
# in NAME order and that order is what CLAUDE.md § RUN IT's *read forward* rests
# on. The minute is a position rather than a clock reading for every entry
# written before the rotation: a historical entry has no time, and two entries
# of one day still have an order.
function is_entry_name(name: str) -> bool
    if !strings.ends_with(text: name, suffix: ".md")
        return false

    if name.len() < 20
        return false
    shape = "nnnn-nn-nn-nnnn-"
    i: i64 @ 0

    while i < shape.len()
        want = shape[i]
        byte = name[i]

        if want == 'n' && (byte < '0' || byte > '9')
            return false

        if want == '-' && byte != '-'
            return false
        i @ i + 1

    rest = name.slice(from: shape.len(), to: name.len() - 3)

    if rest.len() == 0
        return false
    j: i64 @ 0

    while j < rest.len()
        byte = rest[j]
        lower = byte >= 'a' && byte <= 'z'
        digit = byte >= '0' && byte <= '9'

        if !lower && !digit && byte != '-'
            return false
        j @ j + 1

    return true

# EVERY `<map>:NNN` CITATION IN THE TREE RESOLVES, AND WHAT IT RESOLVES TO
# EXISTS.
#
# This is the check CL-037 says cannot exist, and it exists for one reason: the
# map. A line number is unreadable while the thing it points into is prose, and
# readable the moment that line is a path. So the 161 citations that no
# instrument has ever looked at become the 161 this one does.
#
# It asks git for the citations rather than reading every file again:
# `git grep -h -o` over the tracked paths is the same oracle `project_files`
# uses, in one process.
# One citation is written in many places — `beats.md:53` is cited from the
# record and from the map's own neighbourhood — and the same broken line
# reported four times reads as four defects. The offence is the LINE, not the
# mention of it.
function note_once(@offences: [str], text: str)
    if !is_among(text, seen: offences)
        offences @ offences.push(text)

function positions(@r: report.Report, scratch: str, present: [str])
    offences: [str] @ []

    for entry in ROTATED
        fields = strings.words(entry)

        if fields.len() != 3
            continue
        map_path = fields[0]
        stem = strings.without_suffix(text: strings.base_name(map_path), suffix: ".md")
        text = read_file(map_path)

        if text.is_err()
            offences @ offences.push("    cannot read the map " + map_path)
            continue
        rows = strings.lines(text.must())
        cited = shell.run(
            scratch: scratch,
            program: "git",
            words: ["git", "grep", "-h", "-o", "-E", stem + "(\\.md)?:[0-9]+"]
        )

        if cited.is_err()
            offences @ offences.push("    cannot ask git which lines of " + map_path + " are cited")
            continue

        for row in strings.lines(cited.must().out)
            at = strings.index_of(text: row, needle: ":")

            if at.is_err()
                continue
            number = strings.to_number(row.slice(from: at.must() + 1, to: row.len())).default(0)

            if number == 0
                continue

            if number > rows.len()
                note_once(
                    @offences,
                    text: "    " + strings.trimmed(row) + " is past the end of a " + rows.len().to_str() + "-line map"
                )
                continue
            points_at = tree_path_in(row: rows[number - 1], tree: fields[2])

            if points_at != "" && !is_among(points_at, seen: present)
                note_once(
                    @offences,
                    text: "    " + strings.trimmed(row) + " lands on a line naming " + points_at + ", which git does not track"
                )

    if offences.len() > 0
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "records/positions",
            detail: "  a citation by line number does not land where it claims:\n" + offences.join("\n") + "\n  A rotation keeps the map's line count so that these stay true. If one is\n  past the end, the map was appended to; if it lands on a missing file, an\n  entry was moved or deleted after the rotation."
        )
    else
        report.keep_pass(@r)

# No living file names a numbered milestone.
#
# If this fires on a file you just wrote: the identifier is a name now —
# the map is `docs/ROADMAP.md` § The names and the algorithm is CLAUDE.md
# §14. If the file is a dated record, add it to RECORD and say why in the
# commit.
function numbered(@r: report.Report, living: [str])
    offenders: [str] @ []

    for path in living
        text = read_file(path)

        if text.is_err()
            continue
        found = numbered_in(text.must())

        if !found.is_err()
            offenders @ offenders.push("    " + path + ": " + found.must())

    if offenders.len() > 0
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "records/numbered",
            detail: "  a living file names a numbered milestone — §14 says identifiers are names:\n" + offenders.join("\n")
        )
    else
        report.keep_pass(@r)

# Every `M-<name>` in use has a row in the ROADMAP's alias table, and the
# table has the number of rows it had. The map is parsed out of the
# ROADMAP so the table cannot drift from the tree in EITHER direction: a
# new milestone with no row fails here, and so does a row nobody uses
# being the only place a name exists.
function named(@r: report.Report, living: [str])
    table = alias_table()

    if table.len() != ROWS
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "records/table",
            detail: "  the alias table has " + table.len().to_str() + " rows, not " + ROWS.to_str()
        )
    known: {str: bool} @ {}

    for row in table
        known[row] @ true

    for name in REFUSED
        known[name] @ true

    unknown: [str] @ []

    for path in living
        text = read_file(path)

        if text.is_err()
            continue

        for name in names_used(text.must())
            if known[name].is_err()
                unknown @ unknown.push("    " + path + ": " + name)

    if unknown.len() > 0
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "records/names",
            detail: "  a name with no row in the alias table:\n" + unknown.join("\n")
        )
    else
        report.keep_pass(@r)

# Every section of the operating contract a LIVING file points at is a section
# that contract still has.
#
# **This is the third blind spot, and finding it is what turned a renumbering
# into a refusal.** Measured 2026-09-07: `CLAUDE.md` section numbers are cited
# 3280 times across this repository, **2599 of them inside records that §14
# forbids anybody to rewrite** and **346 in commit bodies**, which cannot be
# edited at all. Nothing verified a section number. So renaming `§7` to anything
# would have made every one of those pointers wrong, in silence, with the whole
# net green: the dead-citation check reads paths and stops at the first colon
# followed by a digit, and a section number is neither a path nor a line.
#
# The numbers are therefore permanent, which the contract now says in its own
# § Where the rest lives. What this check holds is the half still movable: a live
# document may not point at a section that is not there. Records are exempt by
# construction, because it walks `living`: a record's pointer is history, and
# §14 has it corrected underneath rather than edited.
#
# Both spellings in use are read, `CLAUDE.md §14` and `CLAUDE.md § 7`. A named
# section, `CLAUDE.md § Hard stops`, carries no number and is not this check's
# business: nothing cites a title but the harness's own comments, and a title is
# free to change.
function sections(@r: report.Report, living: [str])
    text = read_file(CONTRACT)

    if text.is_err()
        report.keep_failure(@r, label: "records/sections", detail: "  " + CONTRACT + " cannot be read")
        return
    present = numbered_headings(text.must())

    # The floor, and it is the shape this check would fail in: a heading style
    # this reader does not recognise leaves the map empty, and every citation in
    # the tree then passes for the wrong reason.
    if present.len() == 0
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "records/sections",
            detail: "  " + CONTRACT + " has no `## N.` heading this reader can see, so every citation in the tree would pass vacuously"
        )
        return
    known: {str: bool} @ {}

    for number in present
        known[number] @ true

    offenders: [str] @ []

    for path in living
        body = read_file(path)

        if body.is_err()
            continue

        for number in unknown_sections(text: body.must(), known: known)
            offenders @ offenders.push("    " + path + ": section " + number)

    if offenders.len() > 0
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "records/sections",
            detail: "  a living file points at a section of " + CONTRACT + " that does not exist:\n" + offenders.join("\n") + "\n  The numbered sections do not move: they are cited 3280 times, 2599 of those\n  inside records nobody may rewrite and 346 in commit bodies. So this is a\n  citation to repair, never a section to renumber."
        )
    else
        report.keep_pass(@r)

# The `## N.` headings of the contract, by number.
function numbered_headings(text: str) -> [str]
    found: [str] @ []

    for row in strings.lines(text)
        if strings.starts_with(text: row, prefix: "## ")
            at = strings.index_of(text: row, needle: ".")

            if !at.is_err() && at.must() > 3
                number = row.slice(from: 3, to: at.must())

                if all_digits(number)
                    found @ found.push(number)

    return found

# The sections a text cites and the contract does not have.
function unknown_sections(text: str, known: {str: bool}) -> [str]
    missing: [str] @ []

    for number in sections_cited(text)
        state = known[number]

        if state.is_err()
            missing @ missing.push(number)

    return missing

# Every contract section a text points at. A byte walk, like every other reader
# in this file, and it has to know that `§` is TWO bytes in UTF-8, 0xC2 0xA7:
# there is no character index here to trust.
function sections_cited(text: str) -> [str]
    found: [str] @ []
    marker = CONTRACT + " "
    i: i64 @ 0

    while i < text.len()
        if text[i] == marker[0] && marked_at(text: text, at: i, needle: marker)
            at: i64 @ i + marker.len()

            if at + 1 < text.len() && text[at] == 194 && text[at + 1] == 167
                at @ at + 2

                if at < text.len() && text[at] == 32
                    at @ at + 1
                last: i64 @ at

                while last < text.len() && is_digit(text[last])
                    last @ last + 1

                if last > at
                    found @ found.push(text.slice(from: at, to: last))
                i @ last
            else
                i @ i + 1
        else
            i @ i + 1

    return found

# Whether `needle` sits at `at`. `strings.index_of` answers the same question
# from the start of the text only, and this walk needs it at an offset.
function marked_at(text: str, at: i64, needle: str) -> bool
    if at + needle.len() > text.len()
        return false
    k: i64 @ 0

    while k < needle.len()
        if text[at + k] != needle[k]
            return false
        k @ k + 1

    return true

# `all_digits` is further down this file, where `item_milestone` already needed
# it: one file is one module, so the second copy this check first carried was a
# `declared_twice` error at the first build, which is the language doing the job
# CLAUDE.md's preamble asks a reader to do.

# No C file lives outside the directories that own C, unless a program beside
# it names it in an `extern`.
#
# This exists because a throwaway that measured `__builtin_classify_type`
# was left in the repository root and `git add -A` committed and pushed it
# (2026-08-14). The list is of DIRECTORIES that own C, not of file names:
# a new runtime part or a fifth spike needs no edit here.
#
# **And a header a program claims is owned by that program** (2026-09-09).
# `examples/gallery/13-lease.hero` teaches text that a C library KEEPS, and no
# library the three platforms share keeps a `const char *` it is handed, so the
# lesson carries its own seven lines of C beside it, as
# `tests/golden/run/lease-c-keeps-the-pointer.h` already did inside a record.
# The cheap repair was to list `examples/gallery/` here, and a directory is
# wider than the fact: the header is legitimate because a `.hero` in the same
# directory says `extern "13-lease.h"`, so that is what is read, and a stray
# `probe.c` in the gallery is still reported.
function c_files(@r: report.Report, living: [str])
    # No leading `./`: `git ls-files` spells a path from the repository root,
    # where `find .` spelled it from the current directory.
    owns: [str] @ ["runtime/", "tools/spike/", "seed/"]
    strays: [str] @ []

    for path in living
        if !strings.ends_with(text: path, suffix: ".c") && !strings.ends_with(text: path, suffix: ".h")
            continue
        owned: bool @ false

        for dir in owns
            if strings.starts_with(text: path, prefix: dir)
                owned @ true

        if !owned && !claimed(path: path, living: living)
            strays @ strays.push("    " + path)

    if strays.len() > 0
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "records/c",
            detail: "  a C file outside the directories that own C, and no program beside it names it in an `extern`:\n" + strays.join("\n")
        )
    else
        report.keep_pass(@r)

# Whether a `.hero` in the same directory as this C file names it in an
# `extern`. Only programs BESIDE the file count: the compiler resolves a quoted
# header next to the program that names it, so a claim from another directory
# would be a claim the build itself does not honour.
function claimed(path: str, living: [str]) -> bool
    header = strings.base_name(path)
    dir = path.slice(from: 0, to: path.len() - header.len())

    for other in living
        if !strings.ends_with(text: other, suffix: ".hero") || !beside(other: other, dir: dir)
            continue
        text = read_file(other)

        if !text.is_err() && names_header(text: text.must(), header: header)
            return true

    return false

# Whether `text` carries a line `extern "<header>"` at column zero, which is
# where every `extern` in this tree stands. A quoted header is the one the
# compiler looks for beside the program; `<header>` is the system's and claims
# nothing here. A mention in a comment or in prose is not a claim.
function names_header(text: str, header: str) -> bool
    for line in strings.lines(text)
        if strings.starts_with(text: line, prefix: "extern \"" + header + "\"")
            return true

    return false

# Whether `other` lives in exactly `dir`, the directory spelled with its
# trailing slash as `claimed` cuts it.
function beside(other: str, dir: str) -> bool
    base = strings.base_name(other)
    return other.slice(from: 0, to: other.len() - base.len()) == dir

test "a C file is claimed by an `extern` at column zero in a program beside it, and by nothing else"
    assert names_header(text: "use strings\n\nextern \"13-lease.h\"\n", header: "13-lease.h")
    assert names_header(text: "extern \"13-lease.h\" link \"m\"\n", header: "13-lease.h")

    # A mention in prose, the system spelling, a different header, and the
    # same header indented, which no `extern` in this tree is.
    assert !names_header(text: "# see `13-lease.h`, beside this file\n", header: "13-lease.h")
    assert !names_header(text: "extern <13-lease.h>\n", header: "13-lease.h")
    assert !names_header(text: "extern \"sqlite3.h\" link \"sqlite3\"\n", header: "13-lease.h")
    assert !names_header(text: "    extern \"13-lease.h\"\n", header: "13-lease.h")

    assert beside(other: "examples/gallery/13-lease.hero", dir: "examples/gallery/")
    assert !beside(other: "examples/13-lease.hero", dir: "examples/gallery/")
    assert !beside(other: "examples/gallery/deeper/13-lease.hero", dir: "examples/gallery/")
    assert !beside(other: "tests/golden/run/13-lease.hero", dir: "examples/gallery/")

# Directories this repository HAS HAD, beside the ones it has. **Panel 086's
# measured hole, closed rather than recorded**: anchoring a claim on directories
# that exist TODAY is self-adjusting in the wrong direction, because a citation
# into a tree that was archived stops being a claim on the very day it most needs
# to be one. Measured 2026-08-19, one commit after the check landed: 6 passed,
# three dead citations unseen. These four are retirements the record already
# documents, and this list grows only when something is retired.
constant RETIRED_ROOTS: [str]
    [
        "crates"
        "docs/debrief"
        "docs/defects"
        "docs/reasoning"
    ]

# Where this repository's prose puts a module when it writes the SHORT name.
# `ir/lower.hero` means `selfhost/ir/lower.hero`, and every document in the tree
# writes it that way; so does `parts/thread.c` for `runtime/parts/thread.c`. A
# checker that does not know the house shorthand shouts at correct prose, which
# is the 52%-false-positive rule panel 086 measured and refused.
constant HOMES: [str]
    [
        ""
        "selfhost/"
        "tests/"
        "tests/harness/"
        "runtime/"
        "examples/"
        "docs/"
        "site/"
        "site/src/"
        "tests/golden/"
        ".claude/skills/"
        "archive/bootstrap-rs/"
        "archive/bootstrap-rs/heroes/"
        "archive/bootstrap-rs/heroes/src/"
        "archive/bootstrap-rs/heroes-cli/"
        "archive/bootstrap-rs/heroes-cli/src/"
    ]

# Whether this line is a human writing to a human, which is the only place a
# CITATION can be. A path inside a string literal is DATA: a `NOSUCH.md` under `docs/` in
# a test is the fixture that proves the compiler says *no such file*, and a
# checker that reads it reports the test as the defect. Measured before this
# rule existed: of 299 candidates in live files, 5 whole classes were code and
# not prose.
function is_prose(document: str, row: str) -> bool
    if strings.ends_with(text: document, suffix: ".md")
        return true
    t = strings.trimmed(row)

    if strings.starts_with(text: t, prefix: "#")
        return true

    if strings.starts_with(text: t, prefix: "//")
        return true

    if strings.starts_with(text: t, prefix: "*")
        return true
    return strings.starts_with(text: t, prefix: "/*")

# The punctuation a citation sits inside, turned to space so `words` can cut it:
# backticks, quotes, brackets and the separators prose puts between two paths.
function unpunctuated(row: str) -> str
    out: [str] @ []

    for ch in row.chars()
        if ch == "`" || ch == "\"" || ch == "'" || ch == "(" || ch == ")" || ch == "[" || ch == "]" || ch == "," || ch == ";"
            out @ out.push(" ")
        else
            out @ out.push(ch)

    return out.join("")

# A citation may end in a line or a line RANGE — `stack.c:202-213`, `x.hero:5:12`
# — and the path claim is the half before the first `:` that is followed by a
# digit. Cutting it is the same move `without_member` makes for `::name`.
# **Byte offsets and not character offsets, and the difference is a panic.**
# `.chars()` counts characters and `slice` cuts bytes; the two agree on ASCII
# and part company on the first accented word in a comment. Measured: this
# function read a character index into a byte slice and the suite died with
# `panic: string slice splits a character` on the real tree.
function without_position(token: str) -> str
    i: i64 @ 0

    while i + 1 < token.len()
        if token[i] == ':' && is_digit(token[i + 1])
            return token.slice(from: 0, to: i)
        i @ i + 1

    return token

# Leading and trailing prose punctuation. A sentence ends and a citation does
# not: `runtime/parts/desc.c.` names a file whose name has no full stop.
function unedged(token: str) -> str
    first: i64 @ 0
    past: i64 @ token.len()

    while first < past && (token[first] == '*' || token[first] == ':')
        first @ first + 1

    while past > first && (token[past - 1] == '.' || token[past - 1] == '*' || token[past - 1] == ':' || token[past - 1] == '?' || token[past - 1] == '!')
        past @ past - 1

    return token.slice(from: first, to: past)

# `syntax/{decl,data}.rs` is TWO claims, and until 2026-09-06 it was none: the
# brace form is one of the two shapes panel 086 measured this check cannot read,
# and it is 26 citations in `selfhost/` alone. Expanding it is not a heuristic —
# it is the shell's own semantics, exactly.
function expanded(token: str) -> [str]
    open_at = strings.index_of(text: token, needle: "{")

    if open_at.is_err()
        return [token]
    close_at = strings.index_of(text: token, needle: "}")

    if close_at.is_err() || close_at.must() < open_at.must()
        # A brace form wrapped across two lines — `emit/{ctype,\n typedefs}.rs`.
        # Half a token is half a promise, and neither half is checkable, so the
        # line says nothing rather than something false.
        return []
    before = token.slice(from: 0, to: open_at.must())
    after = token.slice(from: close_at.must() + 1, to: token.len())
    out: [str] @ []

    for part in strings.split_text(text: token.slice(from: open_at.must() + 1, to: close_at.must()), separator: ",")
        for one in expanded(before + strings.trimmed(part) + after)
            out @ out.push(one)

    return out

# Whether the line carries its own evidence for a path that does not resolve.
# **This is what replaces an exemption list**, and it is not a new rule: it is
# the sentence this check's own failure message already asks for — *if the claim
# is dated history, say so in the sentence and point at where the bytes are
# now*. A list of exempt paths is a second place where truth lives, and this
# repository's record is a long account of second places going stale.
function is_dated(row: str) -> bool
    cs = row.chars()
    i: i64 @ 0

    while i + 9 < cs.len()
        if four_digits(cs, at: i) && cs[i + 4] == "-" && two_digits(cs, at: i + 5) && cs[i + 7] == "-" && two_digits(cs, at: i + 8)
            return true
        i @ i + 1

    return false

function four_digits(cs: [str], at: i64) -> bool
    return two_digits(cs, at: at) && two_digits(cs, at: at + 2)

function two_digits(cs: [str], at: i64) -> bool
    if at + 1 >= cs.len()
        return false

    if cs[at].len() != 1 || !is_digit(cs[at][0])
        return false
    return cs[at + 1].len() == 1 && is_digit(cs[at + 1][0])

# Does this token name something, resolved the way a READER resolves it? From
# the repository root when it starts at one; through the house shorthand
# (`HOMES`); and from the citing file's own directory or any directory above
# it, which is how a relative pointer inside a package is followed. Every
# expansion of a brace form must name something: `syntax/{decl,data}.rs` is a
# promise about two files and half a promise is a broken one.
function names_something(token: str, document: str, known: {str: bool}) -> bool
    for part in expanded(token)
        if !one_names(part: part, document: document, known: known)
            return false

    return true

function one_names(part: str, document: str, known: {str: bool}) -> bool
    if !known[part].is_err()
        return true

    for home in HOMES
        if !known[home + part].is_err()
            return true

    dir: str @ document
    walking: bool @ true

    while walking
        at = last_slash(dir)

        if at.is_err()
            walking @ false
        else
            dir @ dir.slice(from: 0, to: at.must())

            if !known[dir + "/" + part].is_err()
                return true

    # The slug case, and it is the only one that pays a scan: a record cited by
    # its number alone, `docs/panel/042`. Everything above is a map lookup,
    # because `known` carries every directory as well as every file.
    return exists_or_prefixes(token: part, known: known)

function last_slash(text: str) -> i64?
    at: i64? @ fail(code: "none", msg: "no slash")
    i: i64 @ 0

    while i < text.len()
        if text[i] == '/'
            at @ ok(i)
        i @ i + 1

    return at

# Whether this bare word is a path CLAIM: it has a `/`, no glob or angle
# bracket, and its first component is a directory this repository has or HAS
# HAD. Braces are allowed here where `anchored` refuses them, because
# `expanded` reads them exactly.
function is_claim(token: str, roots: {str: bool}) -> bool
    at = strings.index_of(text: token, needle: "/")

    if at.is_err()
        return false

    if strings.starts_with(text: token, prefix: "/") || strings.starts_with(text: token, prefix: "http")
        return false

    for ch in token.chars()
        if ch == " " || ch == "*" || ch == "<" || ch == ">" || ch == "?" || ch == "|" || ch == "\\"
            return false

    head = token.slice(from: 0, to: at.must())

    if !roots[head].is_err()
        return true

    for retired in RETIRED_ROOTS
        if head == retired || strings.starts_with(text: token, prefix: retired + "/")
            return true

    # **Panel 092's half, narrowed to the languages this repository writes its
    # own source in.** A root that has DIED is not a root, so anchoring on
    # today's directories goes green over exactly the citations this check
    # exists for — which is how `commands/flags.rs::FLAGS` sat in `CLAUDE.md`
    # for months. An extension is a fact about the token itself and survives the
    # deletion of everything around it. **`.rs` and `.hero` and not `.h`**,
    # measured 2026-09-06: a `.h` under an unknown root is a C library's header
    # — `sys/stat.h`, `curl/curl.h`, `SDL3/SDL.h` — which is a name in C's world
    # and not a path in this one, and reading them as claims put a whole class
    # of correct prose on the report. **And not `.hero` either**, measured the
    # same hour: a `.hero` under an unknown root is this language's own MODULE
    # PATH being explained — `use syntax/decl`, `geom/point.hero`, `lib/util.hero`
    # — which is vocabulary rather than citation, and reading it as a claim put
    # fourteen correct sentences on the report. `.rs` is left because this
    # repository writes no Rust any more: a `.rs` in live prose is a pointer
    # into the bootstrap and nothing else.
    return strings.ends_with(text: token, suffix: ".rs")

# Every path claim on one line of prose.
function claims_on(row: str, roots: {str: bool}) -> [str]
    found: [str] @ []

    for piece in strings.words(unpunctuated(row))
        token = unedged(without_position(without_member(piece)))

        if is_claim(token: token, roots: roots)
            found @ found.push(token)

    return found

function slashes(token: str) -> i64
    n: i64 @ 0

    for ch in token.chars()
        if ch == "/"
            n @ n + 1

    return n

# EVERY living file may not cite a path that does not exist, and a path that
# does not exist is accepted only when the line carries its own evidence.
#
# **This is the check Linux had to build.** `make refcheckdocs`
# (`scripts/documentation-file-ref-check`) exists because live documentation
# kept citing paths that had moved — a 27-patch *"docs: Fix more broken
# references"* series in 2018 — and panel 086's historian named it as the
# documented failure mode of leaving a citation pointing into an archive.
#
# **IT READ TWO DOCUMENTS UNTIL 2026-09-06 AND WAS GREEN OVER 164 DEAD
# POINTERS.** Measured 2026-09-06: `crates/` appeared 179 times in 145 files under
# `selfhost/` and 19 under `tests/`, eighteen days after that tree became
# `archive/bootstrap-rs/`, and one of the 164 was THIS FILE. Three reasons, and
# each one is a line of code above: it read `design.md` and `CLAUDE.md` only;
# it read backticked tokens only, while the compiler's own citations are bare;
# and it could not read the brace form, which panel 086 measured and recorded as
# a permanent limit — `expanded` reads it exactly now, because shell brace
# expansion is semantics rather than a guess.
#
# **THERE IS NO EXEMPTION LIST, AND THAT IS THE DESIGN.** Four shapes of
# path-like prose are legitimate and would each have wanted a line in one: a
# sentence that says a file is gone and where it went; a placeholder; a
# deliberately absent path in a fixture; a generated artefact. An exemption list
# is a second place where truth lives, and this repository's record is a long
# account of second places going stale — `DECIDE.md` at 391 KB with zero open
# items, a rule written in three documents and performed by none. So instead:
# **the LINE carries its own evidence.** A path that does not resolve is
# accepted when the line holds a date, or another path that does resolve. That
# is not a new rule — it is the sentence this check's own failure message has
# always asked for. Fixtures and generated artefacts fall out for free, because
# `is_prose` reads comments and markdown and never a string literal.
# What git deliberately does NOT track, asked of git rather than guessed at.
# `site/dist/`, `/build`, `.claude/worktrees/`, `site/.cache/` are real things
# this repository writes and refuses to keep, and prose about them is correct
# prose. A checker that calls them dead citations is reading `.gitignore` as a
# list of mistakes. One command, not a list in this file: the list already
# exists and git is the thing that reads it.
# **A path git IGNORES is not a dead citation, and the question goes to git's
# RULES rather than to this disk.** This is the whole of the defect that kept the
# net red on every platform but the one it was written on, from 2026-09-06 22:19
# to 2026-09-07.
#
# The answer used to come from `git ls-files --others --ignored --directory`,
# which lists the ignored paths that HAPPEN TO EXIST. `site/dist/`,
# `site/.cache/` and `.claude/worktrees/` exist on a machine that has built the
# site and made a worktree, so twelve live citations resolved here; a fresh
# checkout has none of them, so the same twelve read as dead and the Linux leg
# failed three runs running while this Mac was green in all three
# configurations. **The machine that wrote the check could not see the defect**,
# which is the shape the platform rules exist for.
#
# `git check-ignore` answers for a path that is not there, measured 2026-09-07:
# `site/dist/index.html` comes back ignored on a tree where nothing under
# `site/dist` was ever built. That is the difference between asking the rule and
# asking the world, and it is CLAUDE.md § 11's narrowing rule in the instrument
# that enforces CLAUDE.md's citations (`docs/contract/case-law.md` CL-004).
#
# **AND EVERY TOKEN IS ASKED TWICE, ONCE WITH A TRAILING SLASH.** The first
# repair replaced the oracle and still asked it the wrong question: it cut the
# Linux failure from twelve dead citations to five, and all five were bare
# directory names. Measured in the container 2026-09-07, on a tree where the
# directories are absent:
#
#     NOT      site/dist            IGNORED  site/dist/
#     NOT      .claude/worktrees    IGNORED  .claude/worktrees/
#
# The reason is in `.gitignore` itself: the patterns are `dist/`, `.cache/` and
# `.claude/worktrees/`, and a trailing slash there means *a directory only*. Git
# decides whether a path IS a directory by looking at it, and it cannot look at
# a path that is not there, so the pattern does not match the bare name. A file
# INSIDE such a directory matched all along, because `site/.cache/key.pem` has
# `.cache` as a directory component in the string itself.
#
# So the token is asked as written and as a directory. Either answer excuses it,
# and neither form can be inferred from the other.
#
# At the end, over only the tokens that survived every other route: a run with
# no dead citation never spawns git at all.
#
# **IN BATCHES, AND WINDOWS IS WHAT SAID SO.** `runtime/parts/run.c` sets
# `HERO_RUN_MAX_ARGS` to 256 and panics past it, so one call per run is a
# promise this function cannot keep: two words per token means 127 tokens is the
# whole budget, and a tree with more unresolved citations than that kills the
# suite instead of reporting them. Measured 2026-09-07 on the Windows box, whose
# checkout sat at an older commit and therefore had plenty:
#
#     panic: a command line reached 256 arguments
#
# **Neither of the other two machines could see it.** The Mac excuses those
# citations before they ever reach here, and the Linux container had five. That
# is the second defect in one afternoon that only the third platform showed, and
# the reason the contract asks for all three before a commit rather than after.
function ignored_by_git(scratch: str, tokens: [str]) -> {str: bool}
    out: {str: bool} @ {}
    at: i64 @ 0

    while at < tokens.len()
        words: [str] @ ["git", "check-ignore"]
        taken: i64 @ 0

        while at < tokens.len() && taken < IGNORE_BATCH
            token = tokens[at]
            words @ words.push(token)

            if !strings.ends_with(text: token, suffix: "/")
                words @ words.push(token + "/")
            taken @ taken + 1
            at @ at + 1

        ran = shell.run(scratch: scratch, program: "git", words: words)

        # **Exit 1 here means *nothing matched*, not *the command failed*.** So
        # the code is not read: only the output is, and a git that could not
        # start leaves the tokens unexcused. The safe direction for a check is
        # to speak.
        if ran.is_err()
            return out

        if !ran.must().started
            return out

        for line in strings.lines(ran.must().out)
            entry = strings.trimmed(line)

            if entry.len() > 0
                out[entry] @ true

    return out

function citations(@r: report.Report, scratch: str, present: [str])
    known: {str: bool} @ {}
    roots: {str: bool} @ {}

    for path in present
        known[path] @ true
        at = strings.index_of(text: path, needle: "/")

        if !at.is_err()
            roots[path.slice(from: 0, to: at.must())] @ true

        # every directory above it, so that naming a directory is a LOOKUP and
        # not a scan over every path the repository has
        dir: str @ path
        walking: bool @ true

        while walking
            cut = last_slash(dir)

            if cut.is_err()
                walking @ false
            else
                dir @ dir.slice(from: 0, to: cut.must())
                known[dir] @ true

    dead: [str] @ []

    # In lockstep with `dead`: entry i of one is the token of entry i of the
    # other, so the git pass below can excuse a line without re-parsing the
    # message written for a human.
    tokens: [str] @ []

    for document in present
        if is_record(document) || is_generated(document)
            continue
        text = read_file(document)

        if text.is_err()
            continue
        number: i64 @ 0

        for row in strings.lines(text.must())
            number @ number + 1

            if !is_prose(document: document, row: row)
                continue
            found = claims_on(row: row, roots: roots)

            if found.len() > 0
                read_the_line(
                    dead: @dead,
                    tokens: @tokens,
                    row: row,
                    found: found,
                    document: document,
                    number: number,
                    known: known
                )

    # **The ignore pass, and it runs LAST on purpose.** Asking git costs a
    # process, so it is asked once, about the handful of tokens that survived
    # every cheaper route, and never at all on a clean run. `ignored_by_git`
    # carries the measurement that made this the last step rather than the
    # first.
    excused = ignored_by_git(scratch: scratch, tokens: tokens)
    speaking: [str] @ []
    at: i64 @ 0

    while at < dead.len()
        token = tokens[at]

        # Either form: `ignored_by_git` asks both and says why.
        if excused[token].is_err() && excused[token + "/"].is_err()
            speaking @ speaking.push(dead[at])
        at @ at + 1

    if speaking.len() > 0
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "records/citations",
            detail: "  a living file cites a path that does not exist:\n" + speaking.join("\n") + "\n  A citation into a moved or deleted file is read as current by whoever follows\n  it. If the file moved, repoint the sentence. If the claim is dated history,\n  the line needs its DATE or a path that still resolves — that is the evidence\n  this check accepts, and it is why there is no list of exemptions to edit."
        )
    else
        report.keep_pass(@r)

# One line's verdict. Split out because `citations` is already a walk inside a
# walk inside a walk, and this is the part with the rule in it.
function read_the_line(@dead: [str], @tokens: [str], row: str, found: [str], document: str, number: i64, known: {str: bool})
    missing: [str] @ []

    for token in found
        if !names_something(token: token, document: document, known: known)
            missing @ missing.push(token)

    if missing.len() == 0
        return

    # **A DATE, and not a living path beside it.** A resolving sibling was
    # accepted as evidence until it was measured: `runtime/parts/run.c` cites a
    # golden that does not exist and names a live file on the same line, so the
    # sibling rule silenced a real dead citation by coincidence. A date is a
    # claim a reader can check against the history; a neighbour is an accident.
    if is_dated(row)
        return

    for token in missing
        dead @ dead.push("    " + document + ":" + number.to_str() + ": " + token)
        tokens @ tokens.push(token)

# **The repository's working text is English** (CLAUDE.md §11), and this is the
# check that says so out loud instead of trusting whoever writes next.
#
# It reads every file that is not a record — the records keep the words they were
# written with (§14) — so a red line here is never history: it is a rule, a
# comment or a page that somebody wrote in Italian today. It takes the whole
# walk and does its own pruning rather than the `living` list, and `APPENDED`
# above says why in full: `living` drops the two files this rule exists for.
#
# **Why per line and not per file.** The shape this catches is one Italian
# sentence inside an English document: a quoted author instruction, a module doc
# citing one, a gloss nobody translated. A file-wide ratio drowns one sentence in
# four hundred English ones, which is exactly the case that got past every reader
# for a month.
function english(@r: report.Report, present: [str])
    found: [str] @ []

    for path in present
        if is_record(path) || is_generated(path) || is_appended(path)
            continue

        if quotes_the_italian(path)
            continue

        if !reads_as_prose(path) || italian_by_design(path)
            continue

        text = read_file(path)

        if text.is_err()
            continue

        for line in strings.lines(text.must())
            if italian_line(line)
                found @ found.push("    " + path + ": " + strings.trimmed(line))

        for span in quoted_spans(text.must())
            if italian_quote(span)
                found @ found.push("    " + path + ", quoted: " + strings.trimmed(span))

    if found.len() > 0
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "records/english",
            detail: "  a living file is written in Italian:\n" + found.join("\n") + "\n  CLAUDE.md §11: everything written is English, and that binds a quoted author\n  instruction too — it is written down for what it MEANT, in English, and the\n  original stays in the git history and in the dated records. If this text is a\n  translation somebody reads on purpose, it belongs under §11's second declared\n  exception and its path goes in ITALIAN_BY_DESIGN with a measurement beside it."
        )
    else
        report.keep_pass(@r)

# Whether this file carries prose a human wrote. An extension list rather than a
# guess at the content: a `.json` lockfile and a `.svg` icon hold neither
# English nor Italian, and reading them buys only the chance of a false word.
function reads_as_prose(path: str) -> bool
    for suffix in [".md", ".hero", ".c", ".h", ".html", ".astro", ".txt"]
        if strings.ends_with(text: path, suffix: suffix)
            return true

    return false

# Prefix match, so a directory covers the files under it — `is_record`'s rule,
# and the `/` guard is why an `italy.md` under `site/src/html/` would not be exempted by an
# entry naming `site/src/html/it`.
# This module itself, by exact name. A suffix match like `quoting`'s would also
# exempt a copy of this file anywhere in the tree; there is one.
function quotes_the_italian(path: str) -> bool
    for entry in QUOTES_THE_ITALIAN
        if path == entry
            return true

    return false

# The record with no directory of its own, kept out by exact name.
function is_appended(path: str) -> bool
    for entry in APPENDED
        if path == entry
            return true

    return false

function italian_by_design(path: str) -> bool
    for entry in ITALIAN_BY_DESIGN
        if path == entry || strings.starts_with(text: path, prefix: entry + "/")
            return true

    return false

function is_marker(word: str) -> bool
    for marker in ITALIAN_MARKERS
        if same_folded(word: word, marker: marker)
            return true

    return false

# ASCII case-folded equality. The fold happens HERE rather than while the word
# is being cut, because cutting builds a word out of slices of the line and a
# slice carries the byte it found: lowercasing at comparison time is the one
# place where the byte is a value and not a position. `Non` and `non` are the
# same marker; the markers themselves are all lowercase.
function same_folded(word: str, marker: str) -> bool
    if word.len() != marker.len()
        return false

    i: i64 @ 0

    while i < word.len()
        b: u8 @ word[i]

        if b >= 65 && b <= 90
            b @ b + 32

        if b != marker[i]
            return false
        i @ i + 1

    return true

# The line's words, split at every byte that is not an ASCII letter. Case is
# left alone here and folded by `same_folded` when a word is compared.
#
# **The inline code spans go first**, before a single word is counted, because
# `site/CLAUDE.md`'s Italian spelling rule is a row of backticked Italian words
# — `dai`, `perch`, `po'` — being ruled ON rather than written IN, and it is the
# one line in the repository that would fire while obeying every rule in it.
# Stripping code spans also drops identifiers, paths and emitted C, none of
# which is prose in any language.
function latin_words(line: str) -> [str]
    out: [str] @ []
    piece: [str] @ []
    inside_code: bool @ false
    i: i64 @ 0

    while i < line.len()
        byte = line[i]

        if byte == 96
            inside_code @ !inside_code

            if piece.len() > 0
                out @ out.push(piece.join(""))
                piece @ []
            i @ i + 1
            continue

        letter: bool @ false

        if !inside_code
            if is_lower(byte) || byte >= 65 && byte <= 90
                piece @ piece.push(line.slice(from: i, to: i + 1))
                letter @ true

        if !letter && piece.len() > 0
            out @ out.push(piece.join(""))
            piece @ []
        i @ i + 1

    if piece.len() > 0
        out @ out.push(piece.join(""))

    return out

# A citation may name what is IN the file — `selfhost/cli/flags.hero::flags()` —
# and the path claim is the half before the `::`. Cutting it is not cosmetic:
# **it is what would have caught the citation that provoked this** (panel 092).
# `CLAUDE.md` cited `commands/flags.rs::FLAGS` for months and this check never
# looked at it, because `anchored` asks whether the first component is a
# directory this repository has (`commands` is not) OR whether the token ends in
# a file extension (`::FLAGS` is not one). Cut the member and the same token ends
# in `.rs`, is anchored by its extension, and is reported dead. Found by a panel
# judge with no `grep`, reading the contract as a stranger would — which is the
# argument for the cut rather than for a wider net.
function without_member(token: str) -> str
    at = strings.index_of(text: token, needle: "::")

    if at.is_err()
        return token
    return token.slice(from: 0, to: at.must())

# Whether the token's last component carries a `.suffix` of one to nine letters or
# digits — `gate.rs`, `ci.yml`, `heroes-spec.md`. Not `docs/panel/042`, and not
# `application/json`.
function has_extension(token: str) -> bool
    cs = token.chars()
    dot: i64 @ -1
    slash: i64 @ -1
    i: i64 @ 0

    while i < cs.len()
        if cs[i] == "."
            dot @ i

        if cs[i] == "/"
            slash @ i
        i @ i + 1

    if dot <= slash + 1 || dot == cs.len() - 1
        return false

    if cs.len() - 1 - dot > 9
        return false
    j: i64 @ dot + 1

    while j < cs.len()
        if cs[j].len() != 1 || !is_lower(cs[j][0]) && !is_digit(cs[j][0])
            return false
        j @ j + 1

    return true

# A token names something if a file IS it, or if a file begins with it — which
# covers a directory (`tests/golden/check/`) and a record cited by its number
# without its slug (`docs/panel/042`).
function exists_or_prefixes(token: str, known: {str: bool}) -> bool
    if !known[token].is_err()
        return true
    trimmed = strings.without_suffix(text: token, suffix: "/")

    for path in keys(known)
        if strings.starts_with(text: path, prefix: trimmed)
            return true

    return false

# No golden's expected output names a milestone in EITHER spelling.
#
# The byte-pinned diagnostic text is where a leak from any diagnostic
# would surface — not only from the emit gate, which is the only one with
# a guard of its own. §4.17's standard: everything needed without opening
# another file, and a milestone identifier is a pointer into a file the
# reader does not have.
function expectations(@r: report.Report, scratch: str)
    none: [str] = []
    listing = shell.list_tree(scratch: scratch, dir: "tests/golden", skip: none)

    if listing.is_err()
        report.keep_failure(@r, label: "records/expected", detail: "  cannot walk tests/golden")
        return
    offenders: [str] @ []

    for path in listing.must()
        if !strings.ends_with(text: path, suffix: ".expected")
            continue
        text = read_file(path)

        if text.is_err()
            continue
        found = numbered_in(text.must())

        if !found.is_err()
            offenders @ offenders.push("    " + path + ": " + found.must())

        for name in names_used(text.must())
            offenders @ offenders.push("    " + path + ": " + name)

    if offenders.len() > 0
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "records/expected",
            detail: "  a pinned diagnostic names a milestone:\n" + offenders.join("\n")
        )
    else
        report.keep_pass(@r)

# The boundary rule, asserted in both directions — the check that keeps
# the two checks above honest.
# EVERY SITTING CARRIES ITS AUTHOR'S VERDICT, AND IT DOES NOT SAY `Pending`.
#
# CLAUDE.md §4 says a panel never blocks: it adopts a conservative default,
# ships it, and queues the author's ratification. That contract has one hole and
# it is not in the rule — it is that NOTHING ASKS. Panels 085, 086 and 087 sat
# unratifiable for four days because no list carried the item; panels 069 to 079
# sat for eight, eleven of them, and were found by accident on 2026-08-24 while
# ratifying three others.
#
# **WHY THIS CHECK ASKS THE VALUE AND NOT THE VOCABULARY** (CLAUDE.md §11's own
# distinction, and this is the measurement that proves it). Five attempts to
# count the unratified by hand that day returned 51, 7, 16, 12 and 11, every one
# wrong differently:
#   - `## Author's verdict` was a recent convention, so old sittings ratified by
#     a DESIGN-LOG line looked unratified;
#   - **the phrase `ratification pending` CONTAINS the string `ratif`**, so a
#     grep for *ratified* counted every unratified sitting as ratified;
#   - `docs/panel/080` says *"no ratification pending"* and matched as pending,
#     its own negation read as the thing it denies;
#   - and a yes is written as *ratified*, *author decision*, *author instruction*
#     or *decided by the author*, across four different files.
# Every one of those failures is a premise about the WORLD — about which words
# the record happens to use. This check rests on a fact about the FILE IN HAND:
# it has exactly one verdict section, and that section does not open with
# `Pending`. A vocabulary can drift; a section either exists or it does not.
#
# The rule has NO EXCEPTION, deliberately. `080` is a retro-record settled before
# its own sitting, and rather than teach the check about retro-records it was
# given a verdict section saying so — a rule with an exception is a rule the next
# exception breaks.
function verdicts(@r: report.Report, present: [str])
    offenders: [str] @ []
    seen: i64 @ 0

    for path in present
        if !is_sitting(path)
            continue
        seen @ seen + 1

        if sitting_number(path).default(0) < VERDICT_FLOOR
            continue
        text = read_file(path)

        if text.is_err()
            report.keep_failure(@r, label: "records/verdicts", detail: "  cannot read " + path)
            return
        pieces = strings.split_text(text: text.must(), separator: VERDICT_HEADING)

        if pieces.len() != 2
            offenders @ offenders.push(
                "    " + path + ": " + (pieces.len() - 1).to_str() + " verdict sections, want exactly 1"
            )
            continue

        if opens_pending(text: pieces[1]) && !queued(path: path)
            offenders @ offenders.push(
                "    " + path + ": the verdict reads `Pending` and no open item in " + DECISIONS + " names it"
            )

    if seen == 0
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "records/verdicts",
            detail: "  `" + SITTINGS + "` holds no numbered sitting, so this check read zero files and\n  would pass whatever the panel had left unratified. Either that tree moved and\n  `is_sitting` did not follow it, or the prefix arithmetic stopped matching.\n  This floor is counted BEFORE the VERDICT_FLOOR filter on purpose: a broken\n  prefix takes every sitting at once, so zero is the shape it makes."
        )
        return

    if offenders.len() > 0
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "records/verdicts",
            detail: "  a sitting has no answer from the author:\n" + offenders.join("\n") + "\n  CLAUDE.md §4: a panel never blocks, it queues the author's ratification — so\n  something has to ask. Give the file one `" + VERDICT_HEADING.slice(from: 1, to: VERDICT_HEADING.len()) + "` section saying what the yes\n  settles and what it does not. A sitting settled BEFORE it ran is a retro-record\n  and says so under the same heading; there is no exception to teach this check."
        )
    else
        report.keep_pass(@r)

# WHERE THE RULE STARTS, AND WHY IT IS A NUMBER.
#
# Measured 2026-08-24, on the check's first run: **57 sittings below 063 carry no
# verdict section at all**, and every one of them is nonetheless RATIFIED — by a
# `DESIGN-LOG.md` line, by the panel watch list (`OPEN-QUESTIONS.md`, retired
# 2026-09-04: ten of its nineteen items were closed, and the file said which
# sittings the author had decided), or by a blanket author
# instruction. They predate the convention rather than break it. CLAUDE.md §14
# says a dated record is not rewritten, so this check does not demand that 57
# files be retrofitted to satisfy an instrument invented after them.
#
# 063 is the floor because it is exact rather than round: every sitting from 063
# up carries a verdict, and no sitting below it does. If that ever stops being
# true the floor moves DOWN with a line saying which sitting moved it — never up,
# because raising it would hide the thing the check exists to find.
constant VERDICT_FLOOR: i64
    63

# The sitting's number, or an error where the name does not start with one.
function sitting_number(path: str) -> i64?
    rest = strings.without_prefix(text: path, prefix: SITTINGS)
    at = strings.index_of(text: rest, needle: "-")

    if at.is_err()
        return fail(code: "unnumbered", msg: "no `-` after the number in " + path)
    return strings.to_number(text: rest.slice(from: 0, to: at.must()))

# **A PENDING VERDICT IS FINE — A PENDING VERDICT NOBODY IS ASKING ABOUT IS NOT**,
# and the difference is the whole defect this check was built for (corrected
# 2026-08-25, hours after the check landed, by the check firing on the panel held
# that evening).
#
# The first version failed on any `Pending`, which made every freshly-held sitting
# a red build until the author answered — and CLAUDE.md §4 says in its own words
# that **a panel never blocks**: it adopts a conservative default, ships it, and
# QUEUES the ratification. A check that contradicts the contract it enforces is
# worse than no check.
#
# What actually went wrong with panels 085-087, and with 069-079 for eight days,
# was never that they were pending. It was that **no list carried the item**, so
# nothing asked. So that is what this asks: a sitting may say `Pending` for as
# long as it likes, provided an OPEN item in `docs/work/DECIDE.md` names it.
# The pair is the invariant; neither half alone is.
#
# **The constant was called `QUEUE` and held `docs/debrief/QUEUE.md`'s neighbour (gone 2026-08-26)
# until 2026-08-26**, which is the confusion the rename fixed one level up: it
# reads the DECISIONS list, and calling it the queue is what let `/panel`'s own
# skill file tell a sitting to append its ratification item to the record instead
# — to the one file this function never opens. A miss here is silent in the worst
# direction: `read_file` fails, `queued` returns false for every sitting, and
# every pending panel is reported as unqueued.
constant DECISIONS: str
    "docs/work/DECIDE.md"

# The list of work that already has a milestone. Read by `homed` below, which is
# the executor of that file's own preamble rule.
# The list of compiler defects that are still open. No instrument opened this
# file at all until 2026-09-07, which is how it came to carry 2753 bytes of
# prose about five defects that had already moved to the record.
constant DEFECTS: str
    "docs/work/DEFECTS.md"

# The line that fences the item region of all three live lists, at the top and
# again at the bottom. It is a CONSTANT for the reason `VERDICT_HEADING` is one:
# the files, the check and the message that explains the check must not be able
# to disagree about it.
constant BANNER: str
    "*******************************************************************************"

# `**OPEN: 41**`, the line under the opening banner. The number is in the file so
# that a reader sees at a glance whether anything is owed, and it is READ here so
# that it cannot be the kind of count that drifts in the copy nobody re-opens.
constant OPEN_PREFIX: str
    "**OPEN: "

# The comprehension list. It takes the same shape as the three above and one
# rule fewer: a ticked question stays in it (`/learn`'s rule — the list is also
# its own record), so `records/lists` reads it with `ticked_allowed`.
constant LEARNING: str
    "docs/learn/LEARN.md"

# The record the three lists empty into. **The same string appears in `RECORD`,
# `QUOTES_THE_OLD_SPELLING` and `APPENDED` above, and it is not the same fact**:
# those three answer *which walks skip this file*, and this one answers *which
# file is the record* — the question `lists` asks it. A record carries one banner
# and no closing fence, because it is appended to forever.
constant RECORD_LIST: str
    "docs/work/DONE.md"

# Does an OPEN item in the queue name this sitting? The number is the key, because
# a panel file's name changes and its number does not (CLAUDE.md §14: an id is
# never renamed once it is in the record).
#
# **THE DIGITS AS THE FILENAME SPELLS THEM, NOT THE INTEGER THEY PARSE TO**
# (repaired 2026-08-25, by this check firing on panel 091 — the first sitting to
# reach it with a pending verdict). It read `"panel " + number.to_str()`, so for
# `091-*.md` it hunted for `panel 91` while every reference in this repository
# writes `panel 091`: measured 2026-08-26, `docs/debrief/DECIDE.md` contains the
# padded form 30-odd times and the unpadded form **zero** times. So `queued`
# could not return true for any sitting between 063 and 099 — the entire live
# range — and the correction that gave this check its second half was inert from
# the hour it landed. It never showed because the only pending sitting since was
# ratified the same night.
#
# CLAUDE.md §11's rule, in one line: the file's own digits are a fact about the
# VALUE IN HAND; how the repository happens to spell a number is a premise about
# the world, and it expired silently. Both forms are accepted because a human
# writing the item may reach for either and neither can name a different sitting.
function queued(path: str) -> bool
    digits = sitting_digits(path)

    if digits.is_err()
        return false
    text = read_file(DECISIONS)

    if text.is_err()
        return false
    padded = "panel " + digits.must()
    bare: str @ padded
    number = strings.to_number(text: digits.must())

    if !number.is_err()
        bare @ "panel " + number.must().to_str()

    for line in strings.lines(text: text.must())
        if strings.starts_with(text: line, prefix: "- [ ] ")
            if strings.contains(text: line, needle: padded) || strings.contains(text: line, needle: bare)
                return true

    return false

# The digits a sitting's filename opens with, exactly as written. Kept apart from
# `sitting_number` because the two answer different questions: the floor needs an
# integer to compare, and the queue needs the spelling the record uses.
function sitting_digits(path: str) -> str?
    rest = strings.without_prefix(text: path, prefix: SITTINGS)
    at = strings.index_of(text: rest, needle: "-")

    if at.is_err()
        return fail(code: "unnumbered", msg: "no `-` after the number in " + path)
    return ok(rest.slice(from: 0, to: at.must()))

# A numbered sitting under `docs/panel/`. The digit is what is asked, and the
# reason is historical: this directory held one unnumbered file until 2026-09-04
# — `OPEN-QUESTIONS.md`, the watch list, which had no resolution and which nobody
# ratified. The rule stays after the file went, so the next unnumbered thing to
# land here is not read as a sitting either.
function is_sitting(path: str) -> bool
    if !strings.starts_with(text: path, prefix: SITTINGS)
        return false

    if !strings.ends_with(text: path, suffix: ".md")
        return false
    rest = path.slice(from: SITTINGS.len(), to: path.len())

    if rest.len() == 0
        return false

    # The BYTE, not a one-character slice: `<` and friends take a number in this
    # language and never a `str` (spec § Operators), which is the first thing
    # this function got wrong.
    first = rest[0]
    return first >= '0' && first <= '9'

# Does this section OPEN with the stub? Only the first non-empty line is asked,
# and the emphasis markers are stepped over, because a real verdict may quote the
# word `pending` while saying what it settles — 089's does, twice. Asking the
# first line asks whether anybody answered; asking the whole section would ask
# what they wrote about, which is a different question and not this one.
function opens_pending(text: str) -> bool
    for line in strings.lines(text: text)
        bare: str @ ""

        for ch in line.chars()
            if ch != "*" && ch != " " && ch != "\t" && ch != "_"
                bare @ bare + ch

        if bare.len() == 0
            continue
        return strings.starts_with(text: bare, prefix: "Pending")

    return true

# **`docs/ROADMAP.md` § Where we are, held to its own ceiling.**
#
# The rule is `/step`'s milestone-close checklist: that section says what is
# NEXT, and a closed milestone's record is its journal, so the section stays at
# 15 lines. It was written with its reason — the file reached 935 lines, growing
# ~66 per close, and a reader met 512 lines about the past before the first line
# about the future.
#
# **It had no executor, and it was broken by 16 lines.** Measured 2026-08-31 at
# M-argv-execution: 31 lines, two long paragraphs of re-measurement history that
# belong to the journals. CLAUDE.md §3 names this exact shape — a rule written in
# three documents that no skill performed, which is how `DECIDE.md` grew to
# 391 KB. A number in a checklist is a wish; a number an instrument reads is a
# rule.
#
# The count is every line from the heading to the `---` that ends the section,
# blank lines included, because a blank line costs a reader the same scroll as
# any other.
#
# **AND THE TERMINATOR IS THE WHOLE LINE, NOT A PREFIX — which is the second
# thing this check got wrong, and the interesting one** (M-thread-stacks step 2,
# 2026-09-06). It asked `starts_with("---")`, and on that day the section held
# **three stacked blocks**: M-isolated-threads' close had APPENDED its table
# instead of replacing the one before it, and the two older blocks had lost their
# `| | |` header rows, so each began with the mangled separator `---|---|`. That
# string starts with `---`. So the count stopped at the first mangled line,
# reported **14**, passed — and **27 lines were invisible to the one instrument
# that exists to see them**. With the terminator read as a whole line the same
# file counts **41** against a ceiling of 15 and goes red, which is what it did.
#
# This is CLAUDE.md §9's shape one level up: a self-check that reads the record
# through a loose pattern can only see what that pattern admits, and the
# corruption it was built to catch is exactly what slipped through it. So there
# are two failures below rather than one. The count is the rule; the malformed
# rule is named on its own because *"41 lines"* would send a reader to trim
# prose, and what is actually wrong is that a table lost its head.
record WhereScan
    counted: i64
    mangled: i64
    found: bool

# The scan itself, over TEXT rather than over the file, so the two shapes it
# judges can be written down as literals in a test (CLAUDE.md §9: every verifier
# check has a test that makes it fire). The version this replaced could only be
# exercised by breaking the real ROADMAP, which is why the defect it missed went
# unnoticed for as long as it existed.
function scan_where(text: str) -> WhereScan
    counted: i64 @ 0
    mangled: i64 @ 0
    inside: bool @ false
    closed: bool @ false

    for row in strings.lines(text)
        if strings.starts_with(text: row, prefix: "## Where we are")
            inside @ true

        if inside && !closed
            bare = strings.trimmed(text: row)

            if bare == "---"
                closed @ true
            else
                counted @ counted + 1

                if strings.starts_with(text: bare, prefix: "---")
                    mangled @ mangled + 1

    return WhereScan(counted: counted, mangled: mangled, found: inside)

function where_we_are(@r: report.Report)
    text = read_file("docs/ROADMAP.md")

    if text.is_err()
        report.keep_failure(@r, label: "records/roadmap", detail: "  docs/ROADMAP.md cannot be read")
        return
    got = scan_where(text.must())

    if !got.found
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "records/roadmap",
            detail: "  docs/ROADMAP.md has no `## Where we are` section, and /step's checklist names one"
        )
        return

    if got.mangled > 0
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "records/roadmap",
            detail: "  § Where we are holds " + got.mangled.to_str() + " line(s) that begin `---` without BEING `---`, and a table whose head is gone reads as a horizontal rule to anything scanning for one. That is how a close appends a second block instead of replacing the first: collapse the section to ONE table, or restore the `| | |` header the mangled line ate"
        )
    else
        report.keep_pass(@r)

    if got.counted > WHERE_CEILING
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "records/roadmap",
            detail: "  § Where we are is " + got.counted.to_str() + " lines and the ceiling is " + WHERE_CEILING.to_str() + " — the ROADMAP says what is NEXT, and a closed milestone's record is its journal (/step, milestone close)"
        )
    else
        report.keep_pass(@r)

# **Every open item in `docs/work/SCHEDULED.md (retired 2026-09-12)` names a milestone that has a row
# in `docs/ROADMAP.md` § The chain and is NOT closed** (author instruction
# 2026-09-04; the rule is in that list's own preamble, this is what performs it).
#
# **It arrived with twelve items of twenty-nine already breaking it.** Nine named
# a waiting condition instead of a milestone — *"the next panel that touches
# emission"*, *"trigger, not a milestone"*, *"the milestone that makes the stack
# guard per-thread"* — and three named a milestone that had CLOSED, one of them
# filed the day before at an id closed 2026-08-12. **A closed home and no home
# read identically in that list**: the item looks scheduled either way. Neither is
# reachable by `/step`, which finds an item by grepping the milestone's name, so
# nine of them were invisible to the one command obliged to open them.
#
# CLAUDE.md §3 tells this story twice already — `DECIDE.md` grew to 391 KB under
# a rule three documents stated and no skill performed. A rule in a preamble is a
# wish; a rule an instrument reads is a rule.
#
# **The first field only.** Everything after the first `|` is prose and may name
# any milestone it likes, closed ones included — that is how an item records the
# home it used to have, which is what several of them now do.
# **AND THE FILE IS NOW THE KEY**, since 2026-09-12. One milestone is one file
# is one lane: the file named for a milestone carries that milestone's reasoning and its open
# items, and two sessions working two milestones write two files with no line in
# common. `docs/work/SCHEDULED.md (retired 2026-09-12)` held all 53 items of 22 milestones in one
# ordered list until that day, so every filing touched the same file and, since
# 2026-09-10, the same ORDER.
#
# What that buys this check is a second question it could not ask before: an
# item's first field must equal the NAME OF THE FILE IT IS IN. In one list an
# item could sit anywhere; here the file is its home, and a mis-filed item is
# one a reader opening that milestone would never see.
function homed(@r: report.Report, present: [str])
    chain = read_file("docs/ROADMAP.md")

    if chain.is_err()
        report.keep_failure(@r, label: "records/homes", detail: "  docs/ROADMAP.md cannot be read")
        return
    is_open: {str: bool} @ {}

    for row in strings.lines(chain.must())
        found = chain_row_name(row)

        if !found.is_err()
            is_open[found.must()] @ !strings.contains(text: row, needle: "| done ")

    files = milestone_files(present)

    if files.len() == 0
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "records/homes",
            detail: "  `" + MILESTONES + "` holds nothing, so this check read zero items and\n  would pass whatever had been filed. Either the tree moved and this reader\n  did not follow it, or every milestone has been closed."
        )
        return

    if is_among("docs/work/SCHEDULED.md", seen: present)
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "records/homes",
            detail: "  `docs/work/SCHEDULED.md` is back, and two places now claim to hold the\n  scheduled work. It became `" + MILESTONES + "` on 2026-09-12, one file per\n  milestone; a list in two homes is the shape this repository's record is a\n  long account of."
        )
        return
    offenders: [str] @ []

    for path in files
        own = strings.without_suffix(text: strings.base_name(path), suffix: ".md")
        text = read_file(path)

        if text.is_err()
            offenders @ offenders.push("    cannot read " + path)
            continue
        state = is_open[own]

        if state.is_err()
            offenders @ offenders.push("    " + own + ": no row in § The chain")

        for row in strings.lines(text.must())
            if !strings.starts_with(text: row, prefix: "- [ ] ")
                continue
            home = item_milestone(row)

            if home.is_err()
                offenders @ offenders.push("    " + path + " names no milestone: " + head_of(row))
                continue

            if home.must() != own
                offenders @ offenders.push("    " + path + " holds an item filed at " + home.must())
                continue
            open_now = is_open[home.must()]

            if open_now.is_err()
                offenders @ offenders.push("    " + home.must() + ": no row in § The chain")
            else if !open_now.must()
                offenders @ offenders.push(
                    "    " + home.must() + ": that milestone is CLOSED and still holds an open item"
                )

    if offenders.len() > 0
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "records/homes",
            detail: "  an item is filed where a reader would not find it — the first field is\n  the milestone whose FILE it lives in, and that milestone has an open row\n  in § The chain:\n" + offenders.join("\n")
        )
    else
        report.keep_pass(@r)

# The one milestone list, and the tree that holds it.
constant MILESTONES: str
    "docs/milestones"

function milestone_files(present: [str]) -> [str]
    out: [str] @ []

    for path in present
        if strings.starts_with(text: path, prefix: MILESTONES + "/") && strings.ends_with(text: path, suffix: ".md")
            out @ out.push(path)

    return out

# `| 35 | **M-declared-freer** | scheduled | …` — the milestone of a CHAIN row.
#
# **The first cell must be a row number**, and that is not pedantry: § Where we
# are opens with `| **Current milestone** | **M-isolated-threads** …` and
# `| **Last closed** | **M-corpus-depth**, 2026-09-04 …`, and neither carries a
# `done` cell — so a looser reader would have learned that the milestone which
# closed this morning is open, and this check would have passed the very item it
# was written for.
function chain_row_name(row: str) -> str?
    if !strings.starts_with(text: row, prefix: "| ")
        return fail(code: "shape", msg: "not a table row")
    rest = strings.without_prefix(text: row, prefix: "| ")
    bar = strings.index_of(text: rest, needle: " | ")

    if bar.is_err()
        return fail(code: "shape", msg: "a row of one cell")

    if !all_digits(rest.slice(from: 0, to: bar.must()))
        return fail(code: "shape", msg: "the first cell is not a row number")
    after = strings.without_prefix(
        text: rest.slice(from: bar.must(), to: rest.len()),
        prefix: " | "
    )

    if !strings.starts_with(text: after, prefix: "**M-")
        return fail(code: "shape", msg: "the second cell is not a milestone")
    name = strings.without_prefix(text: after, prefix: "**")
    end = strings.index_of(text: name, needle: "**")

    if end.is_err()
        return fail(code: "shape", msg: "the name is not closed")
    return ok(name.slice(from: 0, to: end.must()))

# `- [ ] **M-guide-book** | …` — the milestone an OPEN item is scheduled at.
# A ticked item is not read at all: it belongs to `docs/work/DONE.md`, and the
# record is allowed to name a milestone that has since closed.
function item_milestone(row: str) -> str?
    if !strings.starts_with(text: row, prefix: "- [ ] **M-")
        return fail(code: "shape", msg: "the first field is not a milestone")
    rest = strings.without_prefix(text: row, prefix: "- [ ] **")
    end = strings.index_of(text: rest, needle: "**")

    if end.is_err()
        return fail(code: "shape", msg: "the name is not closed")
    return ok(rest.slice(from: 0, to: end.must()))

function all_digits(text: str) -> bool
    if text.len() == 0
        return false
    i: i64 @ 0

    while i < text.len()
        if !is_digit(text[i])
            return false
        i @ i + 1

    return true

# Enough of an offending line to recognise it without printing the item, which
# runs to several thousand characters.
function head_of(row: str) -> str
    if row.len() <= 78
        return row
    return row.slice(from: 0, to: 78) + " …"

# The three live lists hold OPEN items and nothing else — the shape the author
# asked for on 2026-09-07: the items with their state and their description, no
# prose around them, and one loud line where they begin, so that a glance says
# whether anything is owed.
#
# **This is the executor of a rule four documents already stated.** CLAUDE.md §3
# states it, `/decide` states it, `/step` states it, and each file's own preamble
# states it — and nothing performed it, which is CLAUDE.md §3's own story about
# `DECIDE.md` reaching 391 KB told for the third time. What it cost here is
# measured rather than imagined: `DEFECTS.md` had grown 2753 bytes of prose about
# FIVE already-repaired defects, every one of them already in `docs/work/DONE.md`,
# and `DECIDE.md` carried three paragraphs about items it no longer held. A list
# that describes items it does not hold reads exactly like a list that lost one.
#
# Four accusations, and each is a shape one of the three files has been in.
function lists(@r: report.Report, present: [str])
    offenders: [str] @ []

    for path in shell.both(first: [DECISIONS, DEFECTS], second: milestone_files(present))
        text = read_file(path)

        if text.is_err()
            offenders @ offenders.push("    " + path + ": cannot be read")
        else
            for complaint in list_offences(text.must(), ticked_allowed: false)
                offenders @ offenders.push("    " + path + ": " + complaint)

    questions = read_file(LEARNING)

    if questions.is_err()
        offenders @ offenders.push("    " + LEARNING + ": cannot be read")
    else
        for complaint in list_offences(questions.must(), ticked_allowed: true)
            offenders @ offenders.push("    " + LEARNING + ": " + complaint)
    kept = read_file(RECORD_LIST)

    if kept.is_err()
        offenders @ offenders.push("    " + RECORD_LIST + ": cannot be read")
    else
        for complaint in record_offences(kept.must())
            offenders @ offenders.push("    " + RECORD_LIST + ": " + complaint)

    if offenders.len() > 0
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "records/lists",
            detail: "  a work list is not in the shape its own preamble states — a live list holds open items between two banners and nothing else, and the record holds no open item at all:\n" + offenders.join("\n")
        )
    else
        report.keep_pass(@r)

# The record's half, and it is one rule rather than four. `docs/work/DONE.md` is
# appended to forever, so it carries the opening banner alone: a closing fence
# would be a rule the next append breaks, which is the shape this whole check
# exists to stop being written. What it must never hold is an OPEN item — work
# filed among the finished reads as done and is not, and it is invisible to every
# count that asks what is owed, because those count the live lists.
function record_offences(text: str) -> [str]
    out: [str] @ []
    banners: i64 @ 0
    number: i64 @ 0

    for row in strings.lines(text)
        number @ number + 1

        if row == BANNER
            banners @ banners + 1
        else if strings.starts_with(text: row, prefix: "- [ ]")
            out @ out.push(
                "line " + number.to_str() + ": an OPEN item lives in the record: " + head_of(row)
            )

    if banners != 1
        out @ out.push(
            "the record opens with " + banners.to_str() + " banner lines, not 1"
        )
    return out

# One list's text against that shape, as a list of complaints. **Pure, so the
# tests below drive it with the exact lines the defect produced** rather than
# with a file — which is `homed`/`item_milestone`'s division, for the same
# reason: a check nobody has watched fail is a check nobody has watched.
#
# **`ticked_allowed` is `docs/learn/LEARN.md`, and it is one line rather than a
# second function.** A ticked question STAYS in that file by `/learn`'s own rule,
# because the list is also its own record: a question the author has answered is
# a different object from a decision the compiler acted on. So there the first
# accusation is switched off and a `- [x]` is skipped rather than counted, which
# is what keeps the banner's number the OPEN one — what is still unanswered,
# which is the only number that says anything about that file.
function list_offences(text: str, ticked_allowed: bool) -> [str]
    out: [str] @ []
    banners: i64 @ 0
    items: i64 @ 0
    stated: i64 @ 0
    counted: bool @ false
    number: i64 @ 0

    for row in strings.lines(text)
        number @ number + 1

        if row == BANNER
            banners @ banners + 1
            continue

        if strings.starts_with(text: row, prefix: "- [x]")
            if !ticked_allowed
                out @ out.push(
                    "line " + number.to_str() + ": a ticked item lives here, and a ticked item belongs to docs/work/DONE.md"
                )
            continue

        if banners == 1 && strings.starts_with(text: row, prefix: OPEN_PREFIX)
            found = count_stated(row)

            if found.is_err()
                out @ out.push("line " + number.to_str() + ": the count line is not a number: " + head_of(row))
            else
                stated @ found.must()
                counted @ true
            continue

        if strings.starts_with(text: row, prefix: "- [ ] ")
            items @ items + 1

            if banners != 1
                out @ out.push("line " + number.to_str() + ": an item outside the banners")
            continue

        if strings.trimmed(row).len() == 0 || banners == 0
            continue

        if banners == 1
            # An item's body is indented under its line; anything at column zero
            # between the banners is the free prose these files are cleared of.
            if !strings.starts_with(text: row, prefix: "    ")
                out @ out.push("line " + number.to_str() + ": prose between the banners: " + head_of(row))
            continue
        out @ out.push("line " + number.to_str() + ": prose after the closing banner: " + head_of(row))

    if banners != 2
        out @ out.push(
            "the item region is fenced by " + banners.to_str() + " banner lines, not 2"
        )

    if !counted
        out @ out.push("no `" + OPEN_PREFIX + "N**` line under the opening banner")
    else if stated != items
        out @ out.push(
            "the banner says " + stated.to_str() + " open items and " + items.to_str() + " are here"
        )
    return out

# `**OPEN: 41**` — the number the banner states.
function count_stated(row: str) -> i64?
    rest = strings.without_prefix(text: row, prefix: OPEN_PREFIX)
    end = strings.index_of(text: rest, needle: "**")

    if end.is_err()
        return fail(code: "shape", msg: "the count is not closed")
    return strings.to_number(text: rest.slice(from: 0, to: end.must()))

test "a live list in the shape its preamble states has nothing to complain about"
    good = "# T\n\nprose, above the banner, is the rules\n\n" + BANNER + "\n**OPEN: 1**\n\n- [ ] **M-x** | a thing | somewhere\n\n    **Origin:** 2026-09-07.\n\n" + BANNER + "\n"
    assert list_offences(good, ticked_allowed: false).len() == 0

    # And an empty list is a legal shape rather than a suspicious one: two
    # banners, a zero, nothing between them.
    empty = "# T\n\nthe rules\n\n" + BANNER + "\n**OPEN: 0**\n\n" + BANNER + "\n"
    assert list_offences(empty, ticked_allowed: false).len() == 0

test "the four shapes these three files have actually been in"
    # 1. A ticked item in a live list. This is the rule four documents stated and
    # nothing performed, so it is the one shape with no witness of its own — the
    # files were cleared by hand every time somebody happened to look.
    ticked = BANNER + "\n**OPEN: 0**\n\n- [x] **M-x** | done | here\n\n" + BANNER + "\n"
    assert strings.contains(text: list_offences(ticked, ticked_allowed: false)[0], needle: "a ticked item lives here")

    # 2. Prose after the closing banner: `DEFECTS.md`'s five paragraphs about
    # defects that had already moved to the record.
    trailing = BANNER + "\n**OPEN: 0**\n\n" + BANNER + "\n\n**Defect 015 was repaired the same day** and is in the record.\n"
    assert strings.contains(
        text: list_offences(trailing, ticked_allowed: false)[0],
        needle: "prose after the closing banner"
    )

    # 3. A banner missing, which is how a region stops being a region.
    unfenced = BANNER + "\n**OPEN: 1**\n\n- [ ] **M-x** | a thing | somewhere\n"
    assert strings.contains(text: list_offences(unfenced, ticked_allowed: false)[0], needle: "fenced by 1 banner lines")

    # 4. The count disagreeing with the items — the direction an ADDITION takes,
    # which is the one an assert notices least loudly (CLAUDE.md § Commands).
    miscounted = BANNER + "\n**OPEN: 1**\n\n- [ ] **M-a** | one | here\n\n- [ ] **M-b** | two | there\n\n" + BANNER + "\n"
    assert strings.contains(
        text: list_offences(miscounted, ticked_allowed: false)[0],
        needle: "says 1 open items and 2 are here"
    )

test "in the comprehension list a ticked question stays, and the count stays the OPEN one"
    # `/learn`'s rule: that list is also its own record, so a covered question is
    # ticked in place rather than moved. The same text is an offence in a work
    # list and none here, and the banner keeps counting what is unanswered — which
    # is why the flag exists instead of a second copy of this function.
    answered = BANNER + "\n**OPEN: 1**\n\n- [x] **M-ir-lowering.1** | covered | here\n\n- [ ] **M-ir-lowering.2** | still open | there\n\n" + BANNER + "\n"
    assert list_offences(answered, ticked_allowed: true).len() == 0
    assert strings.contains(
        text: list_offences(answered, ticked_allowed: false)[0],
        needle: "a ticked item lives here"
    )

test "prose at column zero between the banners is refused and an indented body is not"
    # The distinction the shape rests on: a body is indented under its item, so
    # it is invisible to no count — the count reads the `- [ ] ` line. Free prose
    # at column zero is what these files were cleared of.
    bodied = BANNER + "\n**OPEN: 1**\n\n- [ ] **M-x** | a thing | somewhere\n\n    **Origin:** 2026-09-07, measured.\n\n    A second paragraph of the same body.\n\n" + BANNER + "\n"
    assert list_offences(bodied, ticked_allowed: false).len() == 0
    loose = BANNER + "\n**OPEN: 1**\n\n- [ ] **M-x** | a thing | somewhere\n\nAnd this paragraph is not indented.\n\n" + BANNER + "\n"
    assert strings.contains(text: list_offences(loose, ticked_allowed: false)[0], needle: "prose between the banners")

test "the record holds no open item, and it is fenced at the top only"
    good = "# DONE\n\nthe rules\n\n" + BANNER + "\n**THE RECORD BEGINS HERE**\n\n- [x] a thing | done | here\n"
    assert record_offences(good).len() == 0

    # The mirror failure of a ticked item in a live list: work filed among the
    # finished, which every count of what is owed walks straight past.
    open_in_record = BANNER + "\n\n- [x] a thing | done | here\n\n- [ ] another | not done | there\n"
    assert strings.contains(text: record_offences(open_in_record)[0], needle: "an OPEN item lives in the record")

    # A closing fence is refused rather than tolerated: the next append would
    # land under it, and the rule would be broken by the act it exists for.
    fenced_twice = BANNER + "\n\n- [x] a thing | done | here\n\n" + BANNER + "\n"
    assert strings.contains(text: record_offences(fenced_twice)[0], needle: "opens with 2 banner lines")

test "the count line is read rather than trusted"
    assert count_stated("**OPEN: 41**").must() == 41
    assert count_stated("**OPEN: 0**").must() == 0
    assert count_stated("**OPEN: 41").is_err()
    assert count_stated("**OPEN: many**").is_err()

test "the section ends at a rule and not at anything that merely starts like one"
    # The shape the record was actually in on 2026-09-06: one good block, then a
    # second whose `| | |` header had been eaten, leaving `---|---|` where a
    # table head belonged. Under `starts_with` that line ended the count at 5 and
    # the check passed over everything below it.
    broken = "## Where we are\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| a | b |\n\n---|---|\n| c | d |\n\n---\n"
    torn = scan_where(broken)
    assert torn.found
    assert torn.mangled == 1
    assert torn.counted == 9

    # The same section with one block: nine lines become six and nothing is
    # mangled, which is what a close is supposed to leave behind.
    whole = "## Where we are\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| a | b |\n\n---\n"
    kept = scan_where(whole)
    assert kept.found
    assert kept.mangled == 0
    assert kept.counted == 6

    # A rule with trailing spaces still ends the section: the terminator is the
    # line's CONTENT, and an invisible space is not a reason to read on.
    assert scan_where("## Where we are\n| a |\n---   \n| b |\n").counted == 2

    # And a file with no such section says so rather than answering zero, which
    # is a different failure with a different message.
    assert !scan_where("# The Heroes Language\n\nprose\n").found

test "a chain row gives up its milestone, and the summary rows do not"
    row = "| 35 | **M-declared-freer** | scheduled | — | — | the freer by name |"
    assert chain_row_name(row).must() == "M-declared-freer"
    assert chain_row_name("| 33 | **M-corpus-depth** | done 2026-09-04 | x | y | z |").must() == "M-corpus-depth"

    # The two § Where we are rows, which is the case that makes the row-number
    # test load-bearing: neither says `done`, and one of them names a milestone
    # that closed.
    assert chain_row_name("| **Last closed** | **M-corpus-depth**, 2026-09-04 |").is_err()
    assert chain_row_name("| **Current milestone** | **M-isolated-threads** — OPEN |").is_err()

    # The alias table, whose rows open with a backtick rather than two stars.
    assert chain_row_name("| `M-day-zero` | M0 | `m0` | git, the workspace |").is_err()
    assert chain_row_name("prose about M-guide-book").is_err()

test "an item's home is its first field, and the prose after the bar is not"
    item = "- [ ] **M-guide-book** | **M-guide-book** (author decision) | the tasks |"
    assert item_milestone(item).must() == "M-guide-book"
    assert item_milestone("- [ ] **M-core-packages** step 0 — three repairs |").must() == "M-core-packages"

    # The nine shapes this check was written for.
    assert item_milestone("- [ ] **The next panel that touches emission** — it rides a sitting |").is_err()
    assert item_milestone("- [ ] **Trigger, not a milestone: the day `grep` returns a generic** |").is_err()

    # A ticked item belongs to the record and is not read here.
    assert item_milestone("- [x] **M-corpus-depth** | closed |").is_err()

test "a row number is digits and nothing else"
    assert all_digits("35")
    assert all_digits("7")
    assert !all_digits("")
    assert !all_digits("3a")
    assert !all_digits(" 35")
    assert !all_digits("**Last closed**")

function boundary(@r: report.Report)
    caught: [str] @ ["scheduled for M8c", "at M5b the increfs", "M0 was day zero", "(M6+)"]
    clean: [str] @ [
        "STAC-M3 Antuco benchmarks"
        "INT64_MIN % -1 does not trap on ARM64"
        "the RM64 encoding"
        "M-selfhost-fixpoint is v1"
        "hero_desc_str"
    ]
    wrong: [str] @ []

    for text in caught
        if numbered_in(text).is_err()
            wrong @ wrong.push("    not caught: " + text)

    for text in clean
        if !numbered_in(text).is_err()
            wrong @ wrong.push("    false positive: " + text)

    if wrong.len() > 0
        report.keep_failure(@r, label: "records/boundary", detail: "  the boundary rule is wrong:\n" + wrong.join("\n"))
    else
        report.keep_pass(@r)

# An `M` followed by a digit at a word boundary — nothing alphanumeric,
# `_` or `-` before the `M`, so `ARM64` is not a milestone and neither is
# `STAC-M3`, kdb+'s benchmark suite: the one genuine false positive a
# word-anchored search hits, and the reason the rule looks behind.
function numbered_in(text: str) -> str?
    i: i64 @ 0

    while i < text.len()
        if text[i] == 77 && opens_at(text: text, at: i) && i + 1 < text.len() && is_digit(text[i + 1])
            # **Four bytes AT MOST, and never across a character.** `slice`
            # aborts when it splits one (spec § 10 Strings, arrays, maps), and the Rust twin cannot:
            # it reaches for `String::from_utf8_lossy`, which replaces a broken
            # byte instead of stopping. Heroes has no lossy conversion, so the
            # window stops at the first byte above ASCII — every byte in the
            # answer is then one character wide. Found 2026-08-19 the first time
            # this walk read a file with an em dash three bytes after an `M8a`.
            last: i64 @ i + 1

            while last < text.len() && last < i + 4 && text[last] < 128
                last @ last + 1

            return ok(text.slice(from: i, to: last))
        i @ i + 1

    return fail(code: "none", msg: "no numbered milestone")

# Every `M-<name>` in the text, with a trailing hyphen left to the prose.
function names_used(text: str) -> [str]
    found: [str] @ []
    i: i64 @ 0

    while i < text.len()
        if text[i] == 77 && opens_at(text: text, at: i) && i + 2 < text.len() && text[i + 1] == 45 && is_lower(text[i + 2])
            last: i64 @ i + 2

            while last < text.len() && (is_lower(text[last]) || text[last] == 45)
                last @ last + 1

            while last > i + 2 && text[last - 1] == 45
                last @ last - 1

            found @ found.push(text.slice(from: i, to: last))
            i @ last
        else
            i @ i + 1

    return found

# Whether this file is one that quotes the retired spelling on purpose.
function quoting(path: str) -> bool
    for name in QUOTES_THE_OLD_SPELLING
        if strings.ends_with(text: path, suffix: name)
            return true

    return false

function opens_at(text: str, at: i64) -> bool
    if at == 0
        return true
    before = text[at - 1]

    if is_lower(before) || is_digit(before)
        return false

    if before >= 65 && before <= 90
        return false
    return before != 95 && before != 45

function is_digit(byte: u8) -> bool
    return byte >= 48 && byte <= 57

function is_lower(byte: u8) -> bool
    return byte >= 97 && byte <= 122

# The names the ROADMAP's alias table carries: every row opening with
# "| `M-".
function alias_table() -> [str]
    text = read_file("docs/ROADMAP.md")

    if text.is_err()
        none: [str] = []
        return none
    rows: [str] @ []

    for row in strings.lines(text.must())
        if strings.starts_with(text: row, prefix: "| `M-")
            rest = strings.without_prefix(text: row, prefix: "| `")
            at = strings.index_of(text: rest, needle: "`")

            if !at.is_err()
                rows @ rows.push(rest.slice(from: 0, to: at.must()))

    return rows

test "a dead citation is caught, and the anchored rule ignores what it must"
    roots: {str: bool} @ {}
    roots["docs"] @ true
    roots["selfhost"] @ true
    known: {str: bool} @ {}
    known["docs/ROADMAP.md"] @ true
    known["docs/panel/086-the-ledger-that-outlives-its-file.md"] @ true
    known["selfhost/main.hero"] @ true

    # A path claim that holds, one that does not.
    assert exists_or_prefixes(token: "docs/ROADMAP.md", known: known)
    assert !exists_or_prefixes(token: "docs/NOSUCH.md", known: known)

    # A record cited by its number, without its slug — the case that would need an
    # allow-list under a stricter rule.
    assert exists_or_prefixes(token: "docs/panel/086", known: known)

    # A directory, with and without its slash.
    assert exists_or_prefixes(token: "selfhost/", known: known)

    # What the rule must NOT read as a path claim: a glob, a bare word, an
    # absolute path and a URL. **A brace form IS one now**, where the old rule
    # refused it: `expanded` reads it exactly, so it is two claims and not zero.
    assert !is_claim(token: "docs/**/*.md", roots: roots)
    assert !is_claim(token: "SPEC_TOKENS", roots: roots)
    assert !is_claim(token: "/usr/include/stdio.h", roots: roots)
    assert !is_claim(token: "https://example.com/a.md", roots: roots)
    assert is_claim(token: "docs/heroes/src/{lexer,syntax}/mod.rs", roots: roots)
    assert expanded("docs/x/{a,b}.rs") == ["docs/x/a.rs", "docs/x/b.rs"]

    # And what it must read: an ordinary citation, and one whose root has DIED,
    # which is the case panel 086 measured this check going green over.
    assert is_claim(token: "docs/ROADMAP.md", roots: roots)
    assert is_claim(token: "selfhost/main.hero", roots: roots)
    assert is_claim(token: "crates/heroes/src/emit/gate.rs", roots: roots)

    # **The tokens no longer come out of the backticks alone**, and that is the
    # repair: the compiler's own citations carry none, which is how 164 files
    # pointed at a moved tree while this check stayed green.
    bare = claims_on(
        row: "# Port of selfhost/main.hero, and `docs/ROADMAP.md` beside it, and plain is not a path",
        roots: roots
    )
    assert bare == ["selfhost/main.hero", "docs/ROADMAP.md"]

    # A line that carries its own evidence: a date, or a path that resolves.
    assert is_dated("# it was `docs/debrief/QUEUE.md` until 2026-08-26")
    assert !is_dated("# it is `docs/debrief/QUEUE.md`")

    # The check itself, firing: a stand-in document citing something absent.
    fired: report.Report @ report.empty()
    citations(@fired, scratch: "build/harness-selftest", present: ["design.md"])
    assert fired.failed + fired.passed == 1

    # **The defect this check shipped with, named after itself** (§9: a fixed
    # defect gets a case). An ignored path that does not exist must be excused,
    # and this asserts it against git's rules rather than against the disk, so it
    # answers the same on a fresh checkout as on a machine that has built the
    # site. `site/dist` is in `.gitignore` and `site/dist/nosuch.html` has never
    # existed anywhere.
    if shell.can_run_scripts()
        excused = ignored_by_git(
            scratch: "build/harness-selftest",
            tokens: ["site/dist/nosuch.html", "site/dist", "CLAUDE.md"]
        )
        assert !excused["site/dist/nosuch.html"].is_err()
        assert excused["CLAUDE.md"].is_err()

        # **The bare directory name, which is the half the first repair missed.**
        # `.gitignore` says `dist/`, a directory-only pattern, and git cannot
        # tell a path is a directory when the path is not there: five citations
        # stayed red on Linux until the token was asked with a slash too. The
        # slashed form is what must come back, and the bare one does not on a
        # tree where `site/dist` has never been built.
        assert !excused["site/dist/"].is_err()

        # And no tokens means no process: the common case pays nothing.
        none: [str] = []
        assert ignored_by_git(scratch: "build/harness-selftest", tokens: none).len() == 0

test "a pointer into a section the contract does not have is caught, and both spellings are read"
    # The two spellings in use, and a bare `§` that belongs to another document.
    assert sections_cited("see CLAUDE.md §7 and CLAUDE.md § 14 for the rest") == ["7", "14"]
    assert sections_cited("design.md §1.6 sets the budget") == []
    assert sections_cited("CLAUDE.md § Hard stops is a name, not a number") == []

    # `§` is two bytes, so a walk that assumed one would read the second of them
    # as the start of the number and answer 167 or nothing.
    assert sections_cited("CLAUDE.md §11 twice, CLAUDE.md §11") == ["11", "11"]

    # The headings, read off the shape the contract actually uses: numbered ones
    # yes, named ones no.
    assert numbered_headings("## 1. Re-read protocol\n## Hard stops\n## 15. Working instructions\n") == ["1", "15"]
    assert numbered_headings("# 3. not a section heading\n") == []

    # The detection firing: a document pointing at a section that is not on the
    # map. It is tested here rather than by writing a broken file into the tree,
    # because the walk would then read that file as a real offender.
    known: {str: bool} @ {}
    known["7"] @ true
    assert unknown_sections(text: "CLAUDE.md §7 is fine", known: known) == []
    assert unknown_sections(text: "CLAUDE.md §16 is not", known: known) == ["16"]

    # And the vacuous-pass floor: a contract whose headings this reader cannot
    # see must fail rather than bless every citation in the tree.
    blind: report.Report @ report.empty()
    sections(@blind, living: ["design.md"])
    assert blind.failed + blind.passed == 1

test "the boundary rule catches the numbers and nothing else"
    assert !numbered_in("scheduled for M8c").is_err()
    assert !numbered_in("at M5b the increfs").is_err()
    assert !numbered_in("M0 was day zero").is_err()
    assert !numbered_in("(M6+)").is_err()
    assert numbered_in("STAC-M3 Antuco benchmarks").is_err()
    assert numbered_in("INT64_MIN % -1 does not trap on ARM64").is_err()
    assert numbered_in("the RM64 encoding").is_err()
    assert numbered_in("M-selfhost-fixpoint is v1").is_err()
    assert numbered_in("hero_desc_str").is_err()

# The check that CLAUDE.md §11 is obeyed, made to fire on purpose (§9: every
# verifier check has a test that makes it fire). The Italian line is the shape
# that got past every reader for a month — one quoted instruction inside an
# English document — and the English lines are the three that would have made
# this check cry wolf: a `constant` full of identifiers, a sentence about the
# Italian edition, and the spelling rule whose subject is Italian words.
test "an Italian line is caught, and the English ones around it are not"
    assert italian_line("non voglio nulla in memoria e voglio tutto nel Claude MD")
    assert italian_line("metti come regola che chi capita sul sito non sia un esperto")
    assert italian_line("Non le voglio le date, non le voglio assolutamente")
    assert !italian_line("Italian nav labels are wider, plus the language badge, so the")
    assert !italian_line("never `dai`; `perche`, `finche`, `trentatre` with the acute accent")
    assert !italian_line("the author studies from the Italian, so where the two diverge")
    assert !italian_line("a b c d e")

    assert italian_by_design("site/src/html/it/index.html")
    assert italian_by_design(".claude/skills/where/italian-vocabulary.md")
    assert !italian_by_design("site/src/html/index.html")
    assert !italian_by_design("CLAUDE.md")

    assert reads_as_prose("CLAUDE.md")
    assert reads_as_prose("selfhost/grammar_expr.hero")
    assert !reads_as_prose("site/package-lock.json")

    assert latin_words("due, tre cose").len() == 3
    assert latin_words("a `token dropped` b").len() == 2
    assert same_folded(word: "Non", marker: "non")
    assert !same_folded(word: "none", marker: "non")

# The quotation rule, on the five shapes the LINE rule could not see: a short
# instruction inside an English sentence. Every one of these was a real span in
# this repository on 2026-09-04, and the line rule missed all five.
test "a short Italian quotation is caught where a per-line count cannot see it"
    assert italian_quote("convoca il panel e finisci")
    assert italian_quote("supera la regola per heroes")
    assert italian_quote("un artefatto di interfaccia")
    assert italian_quote("panel usa sempre soluzione piu robusta e sicura mai piu economica")
    assert italian_quote("usa questa immagine duotonata nella pagina autore")
    assert italian_quote("NO AI SLOP via tutti i")

    # The floor, asserted so that it is a known bound and not a surprise: three
    # words carrying one function word cannot be told from English by counting.
    assert !italian_quote("continua senza chiedermelo")

    # The English quotations this contract and the site rulebook actually carry.
    # Every one of them is a span the check reads on every run.
    assert !italian_quote("this is huge")
    assert !italian_quote("it took 0.94 of the time")
    assert !italian_quote("any textual difference between two versions is SEMANTIC")
    assert !italian_quote("the copy that used to stand here is deleted rather than corrected a third time")
    assert !italian_quote("a suite that had never been run")
    assert !italian_quote("every commit subject cites that path")

    assert quoted_spans("a *\"one two three\"* b").len() == 1
    assert quoted_spans("*\"first\"* and *\"second\"*").len() == 2
    assert quoted_spans("an unterminated *\"span").len() == 0

# **Four markers AND a quarter of the line.** Both, because a long English line
# reaches four markers on its own and a short one reaches a quarter on `una`
# alone. Six words before the line is looked at at all, so a heading, a table
# cell and a nav label are below the horizon.
#
# Measured 2026-09-04 over every tracked `.md`, `.hero`, `.c`, `.h`, `.html`,
# `.astro` and `.txt` file outside the records: **23 files fire, and all 23 are
# declared** — the Italian edition's 22 pages and the `/where` vocabulary. Zero
# false positives, which is the number this threshold was chosen to hit.
# **The quotation rule.** A span of three words or more, inside `*"…"*`, where
# two of the words and a fifth of them are Italian. Lower than the line rule on
# both counts because the span is known to be somebody's quoted words: there is
# no English sentence around them to dilute, and no code to mistake.
#
# Measured 2026-09-04 over every `*"…"*` span in the tree outside the records:
# the only spans it fires on are Italian. **The one span of the 42 it misses is
# `*"continua senza chiedermelo"*`** — three words, one of them a function word,
# so it cannot reach two markers whatever the percentage is. That is the floor
# of this rule rather than a bug in it: a three-word quotation carrying one
# Italian preposition is not distinguishable from an English one by counting,
# and a rule loose enough to take it would take `*"this is huge"*` with it.
# The bound is stated here rather than left to be discovered: **an Italian
# instruction of three words or fewer walks past this check.**
function italian_quote(span: str) -> bool
    words = latin_words(span)

    if words.len() < 3
        return false

    marked: i64 @ 0

    for word in words
        if is_quoted_marker(word)
            marked @ marked + 1

    return marked >= 2 && marked * 100 >= 20 * words.len()

function is_quoted_marker(word: str) -> bool
    for marker in QUOTED_MARKERS
        if same_folded(word: word, marker: marker)
            return true

    return false

# Every `*"…"*` span in a file, in order. A quotation may run over several lines
# — most of the long ones do — so this reads the whole text rather than a line,
# and an unterminated `*"` is simply not a span.
function quoted_spans(text: str) -> [str]
    out: [str] @ []
    i: i64 @ 0

    while i + 1 < text.len()
        if text[i] == 42 && text[i + 1] == 34
            j: i64 @ i + 2
            closed: bool @ false

            while j + 1 < text.len()
                if text[j] == 34 && text[j + 1] == 42
                    closed @ true
                    break
                j @ j + 1

            if closed
                out @ out.push(text.slice(from: i + 2, to: j))
                i @ j + 2
                continue
        i @ i + 1

    return out

function italian_line(line: str) -> bool
    words = latin_words(line)

    if words.len() < 6
        return false

    marked: i64 @ 0

    for word in words
        if is_marker(word)
            marked @ marked + 1

    return marked >= 4 && marked * 100 >= 25 * words.len()

test "names_used reads a name and leaves the prose alone"
    assert names_used("M-harness-port is next") == ["M-harness-port"]
    assert names_used("see M-selfhost-fixpoint — and M-bootstrap-archive").len() == 2

    # A trailing hyphen belongs to the prose, not to the name.
    assert names_used("the M-harness-port- dash")[0] == "M-harness-port"
    assert names_used("ARM-based").len() == 0
    assert names_used("no names here").len() == 0

test "the alias table is read out of the ROADMAP"
    table = alias_table()
    assert table.len() == ROWS
    assert table[0].len() > 2

test "a sitting's queue item is found by the digits the record actually writes"
    # The defect this repaired: `091-*.md` parsed to 91 and hunted for
    # `panel 91`, while every item in the queue writes `panel 091`. The
    # padded form is what the repository uses; the bare one is accepted
    # because a human may reach for it and it names no other sitting.
    assert sitting_digits("docs/panel/091-a-name.md").must() == "091"
    assert sitting_digits("docs/panel/007-a-name.md").must() == "007"

    # The watch list's own name, kept as a case after the file was retired on
    # 2026-09-04: a dashed name with no digits parses to something, and what it
    # parses to must still not read as a sitting number. `is_sitting` is what
    # refuses it, and this line is why that function asks for a digit.
    assert sitting_digits("docs/panel/OPEN-QUESTIONS.md").must() == "OPEN"
    assert sitting_digits("docs/panel/nodash.md").is_err()

    # The integer is still what the floor compares, and the two answers
    # differ exactly where the defect lived.
    assert sitting_number("docs/panel/091-a-name.md").must() == 91
    assert sitting_digits("docs/panel/091-a-name.md").must() != sitting_number("docs/panel/091-a-name.md").must().to_str()

test "the pending rule fires only when nothing is asking"
    # ONE DIRECTION ONLY, AND THE MISSING ONE IS THE LESSON. This case asserted
    # `queued("091-…")` when it was written on 2026-08-25, because panel 091 was
    # pending and its item was open. Both sittings were ratified four hours
    # later, `DECIDE.md` went to zero, and the assertion went red — a premise
    # about the WORLD (what the queue happens to hold) inside a test, which is
    # CLAUDE.md §11's exact shape, written by the person whose job is to police
    # it. What survives is the half that rests on the value in hand: a sitting
    # number nobody has ever held is queued by nothing, whatever the queue says
    # today. The positive direction is exercised on every run by the check
    # itself — it is what refused `docs/panel/091` and `092` until each had an
    # item, which is how the `panel 91` vs `panel 091` defect above was found.
    assert !queued(path: "docs/panel/999-a-sitting-nobody-held.md")

test "a citation that names a member is checked at its file, and that is the hole it closes"
    # The token that provoked panel 092: `CLAUDE.md` carried it for months and
    # this check never looked, because neither half of `anchored` fired — the
    # first component is not a repo directory and `::FLAGS` is not an extension.
    assert without_member("commands/flags.rs::FLAGS") == "commands/flags.rs"
    assert without_member("selfhost/cli/flags.hero::flags()") == "selfhost/cli/flags.hero"
    assert without_member("selfhost/cli/flags.hero") == "selfhost/cli/flags.hero"
    assert without_member("no colons here") == "no colons here"

    # And with the member cut, the dead one anchors on its extension — which is
    # the whole repair: it becomes visible to the check rather than skipped.
    roots: {str: bool} @ {}
    roots["selfhost"] @ true
    assert is_claim(token: without_member("commands/flags.rs::FLAGS"), roots: roots)
    assert !is_claim(token: "commands/flags.rs::FLAGS", roots: roots)
    assert is_claim(token: without_member("selfhost/cli/flags.hero::flags()"), roots: roots)

test "the ROADMAP's § Where we are is counted to its own ceiling"
    # The real file, which is the point: this check exists because the section
    # was 31 lines against a 15-line rule nobody measured.
    r: report.Report @ report.empty()
    where_we_are(@r)
    assert r.failed == 0

    # And the ceiling is a number this file states rather than one it remembers.
    assert WHERE_CEILING == 15

# Whether git is deliberately not tracking this, or anything above it.
# **`is_ignored` stood here until 2026-09-07**, walking a map of the ignored
# paths that existed on the machine running the check. `ignored_by_git` above
# replaces it, and the parameter it needed is gone from `names_something` and
# `one_names` with it: two oracles for one question, and the cheaper one was
# wrong on every machine but the author's.

test "a removal below the record's first line is caught and one above it is not"
    # The shape `git diff HEAD` produces, file header included — those two rows
    # open with the same bytes as a removal and an addition, and a reader that
    # counts them reports the header as a deleted line.
    diffed = "diff --git a/DESIGN-LOG.md b/DESIGN-LOG.md\nindex 1111111..2222222 100644\n--- a/DESIGN-LOG.md\n+++ b/DESIGN-LOG.md\n@@ -3,3 +3,3 @@\n \n-Format: the preamble, reworded\n+Format: the preamble, reworded better\n@@ -240,3 +240,2 @@\n 2026-08-11 | a decision | reason | § | 017\n-2026-08-12 | the decision somebody deleted | reason | § | 018\n 2026-08-13 | another | reason | § | 019\n"
    caught = removed_below(text: diffed, floor: 5)
    assert caught.len() == 1
    assert strings.contains(text: caught[0], needle: "the decision somebody deleted")

    # And the preamble is editable on purpose: `7c2198ff` rewrote eighteen
    # lines of DONE's and was right to.
    assert removed_below(text: diffed, floor: 5000).len() == 0

test "a hunk header gives its old line, and one this cannot read gives zero"
    assert hunk_start("@@ -240,7 +240,9 @@") == 240
    assert hunk_start("@@ -1 +1 @@") == 1

    # The loud direction: zero is below every floor, so a header this cannot
    # read reports every removal in its hunk rather than none.
    assert hunk_start("@@ nothing numeric here") == 0

test "a defect item gives its number, and five shapes that are not one"
    assert defect_number("- [ ] **027 — the title**") == "027"
    assert defect_number("- [x] **014 — repaired the same day**") == "014"

    # A milestone item and a sitting item: the other two lists write their own
    # first field in the same position, and neither issues a defect number.
    assert defect_number("- [ ] **M-core-packages** | work | here") == ""
    assert defect_number("- [x] **panel 131** | ratified | there") == ""

    # Indented, so it is a body line and not an item — every count in this
    # project reads the `- [ ] ` line.
    assert defect_number("    - [ ] **027 — indented**") == ""

    # Two digits and four: the width is part of the shape, because the record
    # writes it padded and a reader sorts on it.
    assert defect_number("- [ ] **27 — two digits**") == ""
    assert defect_number("- [ ] **0271 — four digits**") == ""

test "a number issued twice is reported once, and the record's own exception is not"
    rows = [
        "- [ ] **027 — one**"
        "- [x] **028 — two**"
        "- [x] **027 — the same number again**"
        "- [x] **027 — and a third time**"
    ]
    twice = issued_twice(numbers_issued(rows))
    assert twice.len() == 1
    assert twice[0] == "027"

    # `014` is in the record twice and the record is not rewritten, so the
    # exception is exact rather than a loosened rule.
    doubled = ["- [x] **014 — first**", "- [x] **014 — second**"]
    assert numbers_issued(doubled).len() == 0
    assert issued_twice(numbers_issued(doubled)).len() == 0

test "a rotated record is walked as a tree, and a file is still one file"
    # The names here are deliberately not paths this repository has. A literal
    # that looks like one is read as a CITATION by the check above, and a test
    # about tree walking would fail as a dead pointer — which happened when
    # this one was first written.
    present = [
        "one/file.md"
        "tree/2026-08-04-0000-a.md"
        "tree/2026-08-04-0001-b.md"
        "treeish/not-under-it.md"
    ]
    assert readable(entry: "one/file.md", present: present).len() == 1
    assert readable(entry: "tree", present: present).len() == 2

    # The separator is part of the prefix, or `tree` would swallow `treeish`
    # and the check would read a tree nobody named.
    assert readable(entry: "missing", present: present).len() == 0

test "an entry file carries its day and its place in that day, and six names that do not"
    assert is_entry_name("2026-08-03-0000-the-author-arrived.md")
    assert is_entry_name("2026-09-12-2359-a.md")

    # No minute: two entries of one day would then order by slug, and the order
    # a directory is read in is what `read forward` rests on.
    assert !is_entry_name("2026-08-03-the-author-arrived.md")

    # No date at all, and a date that is not one.
    assert !is_entry_name("the-author-arrived.md")
    assert !is_entry_name("2026-8-3-0000-the-author-arrived.md")

    # Upper case, which sorts before lower case and would put one entry of a
    # day in front of the others.
    assert !is_entry_name("2026-08-03-0000-The-Author.md")

    # `README.md`, and this one is the rule rather than a shape: a directory
    # index is a second place where truth lives, and the only tabulated index in
    # this project drifted for nine closes in silence.
    assert !is_entry_name("README.md")

    # Not markdown at all.
    assert !is_entry_name("2026-08-03-0000-the-author-arrived.txt")

test "a map row hands on the path it names, and a row that names none says so"
    row = "2026-08-03 | pre-M0 | docs/book/beats/2026-08-03-0000-the-author-arrived.md"
    assert tree_path_in(row: row, tree: "docs/book/beats") == "docs/book/beats/2026-08-03-0000-the-author-arrived.md"

    # The preamble names the tree with a trailing slash and no file, which is
    # why the `.md` is required and the prefix alone is not enough.
    preamble = "Every beat is one file in `docs/book/beats/`, and a new beat is a new file."
    assert tree_path_in(row: preamble, tree: "docs/book/beats") == ""
    assert tree_path_in(row: "", tree: "docs/book/beats") == ""

    # THE SHAPE THAT COST THIS FUNCTION ITS FIRST DRAFT. DESIGN-LOG.md's own
    # preamble names its tree and then says `design.md` nine words later, and a
    # reader that ran from the tree's name to the first `.md` sewed the two into
    # one name git does not track — reporting the preamble as a missing entry.
    # It fired correctly and described the wrong thing, which is the worst way
    # for a check to be right.
    sewn = "Every decision is one file in `docs/log/`, still `date | decision | reason | design.md § | panel`."
    assert tree_path_in(row: sewn, tree: "docs/log") == ""

# `rotated` and `positions` are not driven from a `test` block, and the reason
# is worth writing down rather than leaving as an omission: both need the walk,
# the walk needs a scratch directory, and a test that passes `"."` for one makes
# the shell write `stdout`, `stderr` and `listing-project` into the repository
# root. That is what happened here first. They are exercised where they belong,
# by `run_all` over the real tree, and what a literal CAN drive — the name shape
# and the row reader — is driven above.

test "a paths glob is asked for the part before its star"
    assert prefix_of("docs/**") == "docs"
    assert prefix_of("selfhost/emit/ffi*") == "selfhost/emit/ffi"
    assert prefix_of("CLAUDE.md") == "CLAUDE.md"

    # A partial file name is a PREFIX and not a tree, which is why the matcher
    # beside it is not `selects_tree`: `selfhost/emit/ffi` names no directory and
    # reaches twelve files.
    present = ["one/ffi_gate.hero", "one/other.hero"]
    assert selects_prefix(entry: "one/ffi", present: present)
    assert !selects_prefix(entry: "one/zzz", present: present)

test "a quoted glob loses its quotes and an unquoted one is unchanged"
    assert unquoted("\"docs/**\"") == "docs/**"
    assert unquoted("docs/**") == "docs/**"
